WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:08.850
- Welcome everybody.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:10.800
Today we have the pleasure

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.570
of having Dr. Paula López Peña,

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:17.070
who is a postdoc with us

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.383
in the refugee program here at Yale,

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:24.140
presenting her work on the Causes

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:27.913
and Consequences of Ethnic
Violence in Myanmar.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:34.280
Dr. López Peña has been teaching here

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:38.670
at the Departments of Economics.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:41.780
And her areas of research
are applied microeconomics

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:43.900
and development economics.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:47.470
She studies how non-cognitive skills

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:49.840
and adverse life events affect health

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.900
and labor market outcomes.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.000
Her current projects
focus on entrepreneurship,

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.210
firm productivity, and the causes

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.350
and consequences of violence.

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:01.360
So thank you very much Paula,

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:02.963
and the floor is yours.

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:07.020
- Thank you very much
Teresa for the introduction,

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.870
and also for hosting this event.

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:14.530
We are delighted to be
presenting our work on the causes

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:18.400
and consequences of ethnic
violence in Myanmar today.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.730
And here, the caveat is,

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:23.250
today, we're gonna be presenting results

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:27.900
from the decade prior to the
beginning of the pandemic.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.150
My coauthors are here with me today.

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:34.010
Austin Davis from American University,

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:35.560
Mushfiq Mobarak from Yale,

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:39.003
and Jaya Wen from the
Harvard Business School.

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:41.120
We're all looking forward

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.940
to having your feedback and comments.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:47.410
Feel free to ask me questions
directly at any time.

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:48.923
Just unmute yourself.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:50.593
Or if you prefer,

00:01:50.593 --> 00:01:53.290
you can just write your
question on the chat box

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:57.023
and one of us will answer your
question as soon as possible.

00:01:58.529 --> 00:01:59.362
And with that,

00:01:59.362 --> 00:02:00.780
I'm gonna get started.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:05.780
And I want to start by briefly
introducing the context

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:11.380
for the persecution of
Rohingya movements in Myanmar.

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:12.550
As many of you may know,

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:15.000
Myanmar is one of the most

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.370
ethnically diverse countries on earth.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:20.980
And almost since its foundation,

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:25.060
it has been torn apart by armed clashes

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:28.030
between the different ethnic militias

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:31.453
and the state's Armed
Forces, the Tatmadaw.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:34.760
And of all ethnic groups,

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:37.110
the Rohingya Muslims who start out

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:42.070
for having been the most
severely persecuted minority.

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:46.240
They were stripped of citizenship in 1982.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:47.730
And ever since then,

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:50.640
they have been relentlessly harassed,

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:52.200
both by the Tatmadaw

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.120
and sometimes by their
own neighbors next door.

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:58.630
Furthermore, there is growing evidence

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:01.090
that there has been a sharp increase

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:05.630
in the number of violent events
started in Rohingya Muslims,

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:08.260
since 2011, which was the year

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:11.500
in which Myanmar started as a country,

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.373
a transitioning to democracy.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.400
The latest outbreak of violence

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:21.400
the infamous military crackdown
that took place in 2017,

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:26.350
alone, displaced over
700,000 Rohingya Muslims,

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:30.320
the vast majority of whom are
currently living in Bangladesh

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:31.893
in crowded refugee camps.

00:03:34.149 --> 00:03:34.982
One of the facts

00:03:34.982 --> 00:03:37.230
that we're going to present
to you today is that,

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:42.230
very large proportions of the
refugees living in Cox's Bazar

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.700
is that the vast majority of them

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:48.710
report having survived

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:53.710
or witnessed physical
assault, murder, rape,

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:54.973
and arson attacks.

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:59.410
And despite how pervasive
these narratives are,

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:01.310
what happened exactly

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:05.300
in the aftermath of the 2017 crackdown,

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:06.640
and why it happened,

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:08.323
remains highly contested.

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:13.320
And our paper will speak directly

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.810
to the two predominant narratives

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:18.453
and explanations for
what happened and why.

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:22.420
The first is that of the
international community,

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:26.210
which primarily agrees
that the kind of violence

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:28.570
that the Rohingya have been exposed to,

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:32.010
especially during and after 2017,

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:35.173
are a textbook example
of ethnic cleansing.

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:39.470
And that the evidence of mass killings

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:42.470
and mass gang rapes that
they have been exposed to

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:47.470
show a clear genocidal intent
on the side of the military.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:48.780
On the other hand,

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:50.090
the compelling narrative

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:54.160
is that of the former civilian
government of Myanmar,

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:55.780
now the post,

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:59.030
former State Councilor Aung San Suu Kyi

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:01.550
declared that it was still not easy

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:04.320
to establish clear patterns of events,

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:05.320
and that it's possible

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:08.840
that some refugees might
have provided inaccurate

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:10.960
or exaggerated information.

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:14.910
So the bottom line is the
government of Myanmar argues

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:16.630
that there is no evidence

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:18.750
that the persecution of Rohingya Muslims

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:22.600
or violence against Rohingya
Muslims has been widespread,

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:23.850
or systematic.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:30.220
Our goal with this paper is to
provide quantitative evidence

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:33.700
to complement the existing
qualitative narratives,

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:36.970
those that have been told
by refugees themselves.

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:39.990
and reported by numerous
journalistic accounts

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:41.623
from reporters in the field.

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:45.760
And the two main contributions
that these paper makes,

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:47.090
is, on the one hand,

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:50.750
we're gonna exploit a
very detailed data set

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:53.900
that contains daily
data on conflict events

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:57.320
from across Myanmar for over a decade.

00:05:57.320 --> 00:05:59.660
And this makes our conclusions

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:01.620
less susceptible to cherry-picking,

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:04.800
and less susceptible
to perhaps over-reliant

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:08.720
on arguably inaccurate recollections.

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:09.860
And in doing so,

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:11.690
what we help us to provide

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:15.860
a descriptive in-depth set of facts

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:19.690
indicative of a long
term, mythological pattern

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:21.320
on the side of the military,

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:24.410
and how the military
treats differently Rohingya

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:26.470
from other ethnic minorities.

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:29.390
And importantly, our data will allow us

00:06:29.390 --> 00:06:32.530
to tell you a causal story

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:35.450
on one important driver of violence.

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:38.410
And our focus here is going to be that,

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:41.210
violence has economic motivations.

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:44.926
And this side of the story
has often been overlooked.

00:06:44.926 --> 00:06:45.759
(mumbles)

00:06:45.759 --> 00:06:46.592
In many instances,

00:06:46.592 --> 00:06:48.900
the conflict has been characterized

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:53.560
as being under underlined by
deep-rooted religious hatred,

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:55.043
or ethnic tensions.

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:57.400
In doing so,

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:00.530
we're gonna contribute to a
very broad economics literature

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:03.430
on ethnic conflict and
interstate conflict.

00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:07.380
But our goal with this
paper was also to try

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:10.360
to contribute to the ongoing policy debate

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:12.830
on how the international community

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:16.250
could potentially help
prevent the military

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:18.410
or decentralize the military

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:22.580
from continue to engage in
violence against civilians,

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:26.500
and perhaps how to inform
the humanitarian response

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:28.470
to address the psychological toll

00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:30.270
that these refugees bring with them.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:35.530
The response that we're
gonna present to you,

00:07:35.530 --> 00:07:37.430
and our story,

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:41.590
is a structure around a
three-prong empirical approach.

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:43.620
Firstly, we're going to present you

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:46.840
a set of difference-in-difference
regressions

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:49.050
that are going to help us establish

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:52.800
whether or not there were
systematic economic motivations

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:55.170
driving ethnic persecution in Myanmar.

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:58.700
And the hypothesis we're
going to test is that,

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:03.050
in periods when rice prices
are particularly high,

00:08:03.050 --> 00:08:07.140
in regions that are more
suitable for rice cultivation,

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:08.510
conflict might increase.

00:08:08.510 --> 00:08:09.910
And if this is the case,

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:10.743
this is evidence that

00:08:10.743 --> 00:08:14.670
there are systematic economic
motivations behind violence.

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:17.630
The second part of the
paper is going to be based

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:20.553
on vector auto-regressions, or VARs.

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:23.840
This is a statistical framework

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:25.880
that allows us to document

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:29.640
how different actors
involved in this conflict

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:31.770
behave over time,

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:34.810
and how they respond to
one another's action.

00:08:34.810 --> 00:08:38.280
And this is gonna be very
helpful for us to compare

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:41.500
the way in which the military
has treated the Rohingya

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:43.303
compared to other ethnic minorities.

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:46.220
And lastly, we're gonna present you

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:48.400
a set of very basic

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:51.390
yet hard to come by summary and statistics

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:53.580
on the prevalence of depression

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:55.960
and past experiences with trauma

00:08:55.960 --> 00:08:59.600
among refugees currently
living in Cox's Bazar.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:04.130
And this is to try to pay
the enormous human toll

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:05.640
of the ethnic persecution

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:08.203
we're trying to describe and explain.

00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:12.010
And we're going to start
with the first section,

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:14.720
the rice differences-in-differences
approach.

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:17.710
Here, as I was saying earlier,

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:21.270
the motivation or the
goal is to understand

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:26.270
whether changes in our
international price of rice

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:27.900
could lead to an increase

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:29.240
or a decrease in conflict

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:32.540
in areas that are more
suitable for land cultivation,

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:34.460
which would then suggest

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:37.803
that ethnic violence is
underlined by economic markets.

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:41.530
And our empirical analysis
is going to be based

00:09:41.530 --> 00:09:43.060
on a series of stylized facts

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:45.810
that make it particularly suitable

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:47.733
for asking the question at hand.

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:49.433
The first is that,

00:09:49.433 --> 00:09:53.150
rice is the most important
staple food in Myanmar.

00:09:53.150 --> 00:09:57.260
It accounts for over 40%
of agricultural production.

00:09:57.260 --> 00:09:58.660
And in particular,

00:09:58.660 --> 00:10:02.940
most of the rice cultivated
the country is wetland rice,

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:05.440
which you can see portrayed in the picture

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:07.183
on the bottom left of this slide.

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:11.920
Secondly, Myanmar is a price taker

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:13.890
on the international global market.

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:16.600
Even though it exports
a very large fraction

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:19.530
of its domestic crop, nearly 10%,

00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:22.110
the amount is still too small

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:24.820
to affect international rice prices.

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:28.600
And therefore this is going
to be a core assumption

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:30.410
underlying our analysis.

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:32.060
Myanmar is a price-taker,

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:34.320
and hence changes in international prices

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:37.190
are going to be active
as an exogenous shock

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:38.453
to domestic prices.

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:43.411
The last stylized fact that
underlines our analysis

00:10:43.411 --> 00:10:45.140
and that it is going to help us

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:50.140
identify the causal effect of
changes in prices is the fact

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:54.923
that wetland like rice has very
specific geoclimatic needs.

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:58.250
If you look at a map of Myanmar,

00:10:58.250 --> 00:10:59.600
and even within Rakhine State,

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:01.420
what you're gonna see is that

00:11:01.420 --> 00:11:03.500
the suitability of different regions

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:04.690
both within the country

00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:06.160
and within the state,

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:09.400
for growing rice is gonna vary widely.

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:12.840
Here what we present is
an index of suitability

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:14.550
of different plots of land,

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:16.210
both in Myanmar as a whole

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:18.197
and Rakhine where the Rohingya live.

00:11:18.197 --> 00:11:22.350
And what this shows is that
there is a lot of variation

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:24.690
in how suitable some
places are for growing rice

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:26.300
relative to others.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:29.590
And here we're gonna
exploit a very rich data set

00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:31.100
coming from the FAO,

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:34.400
the Global Agro-Ecological Zone data set

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:38.190
that contains very fine
granular information

00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:41.020
on how good a certain area is

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:43.500
for cultivating and growing rice.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:44.910
And did I mention,

00:11:44.910 --> 00:11:47.970
suitability is going to be
composed by a number of variables

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:52.970
such as precipitation,
temperature, quality soil, etc.

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:58.910
Now, our empirical strategy is designed

00:11:58.910 --> 00:12:01.850
to test two potential mechanisms

00:12:01.850 --> 00:12:06.850
through which changes in rice
prices could affect conflict.

00:12:07.180 --> 00:12:10.770
The first is the so
called rapacity effect.

00:12:10.770 --> 00:12:12.940
What this tells us is that,

00:12:12.940 --> 00:12:16.670
if the price of a commodity goes up,

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:19.530
that means that the commodity
becomes more valuable.

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:22.470
And therefore, this creates incentives

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:25.550
for people to try to
appropriate the commodity,

00:12:25.550 --> 00:12:28.580
or even the inputs utilized

00:12:28.580 --> 00:12:30.170
to produce that commodity.

00:12:30.170 --> 00:12:32.390
In our context, that would be land.

00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:34.350
So in this case,

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:37.410
a positive shock to the price of rice

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:39.150
would increase conflict

00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:43.563
in areas that are particularly
suitable for growing rice.

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:46.153
But at the same time,

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:50.000
rice is a very labor-intensive crop.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:52.560
It requires a lot of manpower,

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:54.900
and a lot of human effort.

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:57.590
And therefore, it's possible

00:12:57.590 --> 00:13:01.470
that if the price of
the commodity goes up,

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:06.020
the salary of agricultural
workers growing it increases.

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:09.150
And these positive shock to labor income

00:13:09.150 --> 00:13:11.480
acts as a disincentive

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:13.450
to engage in other economic activities

00:13:13.450 --> 00:13:14.700
that might cost time.

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:16.870
For instance, engaging in appropriating

00:13:16.870 --> 00:13:19.210
or looting rice from someone else,

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:21.710
which might also have some form of cost,

00:13:21.710 --> 00:13:23.150
some form of punishment.

00:13:23.150 --> 00:13:24.480
So in this case,

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:27.680
this is what we call the
opportunity cost mechanism.

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:28.520
In this case,

00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:33.310
a positive shock to rice
prices has a pacifying effect.

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:35.060
And what is interesting

00:13:35.060 --> 00:13:39.733
about analyzing the effect
of shocks to rice prices,

00:13:40.890 --> 00:13:42.350
is that it could go either way.

00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:45.680
It's possible that there
is a rapacity effect

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:46.513
in some places,

00:13:46.513 --> 00:13:48.910
and an opportunity costs in other places.

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:52.580
And here is when it becomes interesting

00:13:52.580 --> 00:13:54.610
that we're studying Myanmar.

00:13:54.610 --> 00:13:55.890
Myanmar is the setting

00:13:55.890 --> 00:13:59.090
where one of the conflict actors

00:13:59.090 --> 00:14:02.940
has a much stronger military capability

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:05.650
than all other actors that we're studying,

00:14:05.650 --> 00:14:08.520
than any other ethnic militia.

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:12.350
And therefore what we are
going to argue is that,

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:13.740
in our setting,

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:16.890
these forces might be particularly strong,

00:14:16.890 --> 00:14:20.230
especially in areas
that are highly suitable

00:14:20.230 --> 00:14:21.620
for land cultivation.

00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:24.723
And these makes ideal our
difference-in-difference set up.

00:14:26.964 --> 00:14:28.650
This is our baseline specification.

00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:31.640
This is the regression
that we're gonna run.

00:14:31.640 --> 00:14:32.710
To begin with,

00:14:32.710 --> 00:14:34.050
across the whole country

00:14:34.050 --> 00:14:37.290
using conflict data from across Myanmar.

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:40.870
Our outcomes of interest are described

00:14:40.870 --> 00:14:43.400
on the left hand side, the y's.

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:48.220
We're gonna focus on three
subsets of conflict outcomes.

00:14:48.220 --> 00:14:50.370
The first is violence against civilians

00:14:50.370 --> 00:14:54.000
perpetrated by any conflict
actor in the country.

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:57.360
The second is a subset of that outcome,

00:14:57.360 --> 00:14:58.980
violence against civilians,

00:14:58.980 --> 00:15:01.580
in this case, perpetrated by state forces.

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:06.290
And the last is gonna be
looting or property damage.

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:08.870
And on the right hand
side of the regression,

00:15:08.870 --> 00:15:11.533
we're gonna have an interaction term.

00:15:12.440 --> 00:15:16.240
The first component of
which is land suitability.

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:18.840
And this measure summarizes

00:15:18.840 --> 00:15:23.400
how good a certain
township is, to grow rice.

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:25.640
So this is the average quality

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:28.460
or the average level
of suitability of land

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:29.970
in a certain township

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:31.323
to cultivate rice.

00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:36.920
And this is gonna be multiplied
by an index of rice prices.

00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:40.060
And here, what we do is
aggregating rice prices

00:15:40.060 --> 00:15:42.440
for the three most commonly grown

00:15:42.440 --> 00:15:46.230
and sold types of rices
domestically in Myanmar

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:47.740
on a certain month year.

00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:49.450
So these will be the equivalent

00:15:49.450 --> 00:15:50.830
to thinking about rice,

00:15:50.830 --> 00:15:52.490
or the average price of rice,

00:15:52.490 --> 00:15:56.040
for instance, in January 2019.

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:59.130
And then four, the
coefficient of interest,

00:15:59.130 --> 00:16:02.400
and the one which we're gonna show to you

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:04.770
in a couple of slides, is beta.

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:06.360
The one that is gonna capture

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:09.740
whether an increase in the price of rice

00:16:09.740 --> 00:16:11.650
is going to lead to increased conflict

00:16:11.650 --> 00:16:15.730
in areas that are more
suitable for land cultivation.

00:16:15.730 --> 00:16:20.320
Now, we're gonna also add a
vector of control variables

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:22.970
just to ensure that the fact

00:16:22.970 --> 00:16:26.310
that some months are always
different every year,

00:16:26.310 --> 00:16:28.640
and that some townships are different

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:32.100
across the decade that we observed,

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:36.070
that these two effects
don't bias our estimates.

00:16:36.070 --> 00:16:38.660
And we're also going to add some controls

00:16:38.660 --> 00:16:40.670
for latitude and longitude,

00:16:40.670 --> 00:16:42.770
as well as a dummy variable

00:16:42.770 --> 00:16:46.453
to control for whether a
township is coastal or not.

00:16:48.010 --> 00:16:53.010
And lastly, we are going to
instrument domestic prices

00:16:53.260 --> 00:16:55.520
using international prices.

00:16:55.520 --> 00:16:57.270
And this is to address

00:16:57.270 --> 00:16:59.660
a potentially important
source of endogeneity

00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:00.493
in our setting,

00:17:00.493 --> 00:17:02.540
which is, it's possible

00:17:02.540 --> 00:17:04.070
or we have reasons to believe

00:17:04.070 --> 00:17:06.870
that the level of conflict
in a certain township

00:17:06.870 --> 00:17:10.950
could affect the price
of rice domestically.

00:17:10.950 --> 00:17:13.360
But we think it's highly unlikely

00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:15.680
that conflict in a certain
township in Myanmar

00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:18.320
is gonna have an effect
on international prices.

00:17:18.320 --> 00:17:21.500
And that's what makes this
a very good instrument.

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:24.985
Reassuringly, we've
tested for the correlation

00:17:24.985 --> 00:17:26.900
between these two variables.

00:17:26.900 --> 00:17:27.760
It's very high,

00:17:27.760 --> 00:17:31.160
so we have a very strong (mumbles)

00:17:31.160 --> 00:17:35.610
And just to assume into how
we wanna interpret the beta,

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:38.243
why beta is the coefficient of interest.

00:17:38.243 --> 00:17:41.160
This is because beta is going to allow us

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:43.460
to test our null hypothesis,

00:17:43.460 --> 00:17:45.880
which is that economic forces

00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:49.320
do not drive ethnic conflict in Myanmar.

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:51.970
And hence, both the magnitude

00:17:51.970 --> 00:17:54.020
and the significance of beta

00:17:54.020 --> 00:17:57.360
contain important information for us.

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:00.160
If beta is very close to zero,

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:03.330
not statistically significantly
different from zero,

00:18:03.330 --> 00:18:07.660
then we would not be able
to argue based on our data,

00:18:07.660 --> 00:18:10.530
that there exists
systematic economic forces

00:18:10.530 --> 00:18:13.730
driving violence behind the scenes.

00:18:13.730 --> 00:18:17.130
However, if beta is different from zero,

00:18:17.130 --> 00:18:20.520
regardless of whether
it's positive or negative,

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:22.150
we take that as evidence

00:18:22.150 --> 00:18:23.750
that there are economic motivations

00:18:23.750 --> 00:18:27.100
driving ethnic violence in Myanmar.

00:18:27.100 --> 00:18:30.460
And in particular, if beta is negative,

00:18:30.460 --> 00:18:32.193
what this tells us is that,

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:37.145
an increase in rice prices is gonna lead

00:18:37.145 --> 00:18:38.880
to a decrease in conflict

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:42.210
in areas that are more
suitable for land cultivation,

00:18:42.210 --> 00:18:43.680
therefore, indicating that

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:46.340
there are economic forces
driving ethnic conflict

00:18:46.340 --> 00:18:50.590
and that the opportunity
cost mechanism dominates.

00:18:50.590 --> 00:18:52.363
So the shock is pacifying.

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:56.770
Conversely, if beta was positive,

00:18:56.770 --> 00:18:58.500
what this indicates is that

00:18:58.500 --> 00:19:02.300
in response to a positive
shock to international prices,

00:19:02.300 --> 00:19:04.380
conflict is higher in areas

00:19:04.380 --> 00:19:06.650
that are more suitable
for land cultivation.

00:19:06.650 --> 00:19:07.680
And what this means is that

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:10.770
the rapacity mechanism dominates.

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:12.130
So the bottom line

00:19:12.130 --> 00:19:14.830
before jumping on the main results table,

00:19:14.830 --> 00:19:17.030
beta contains important information

00:19:17.030 --> 00:19:19.983
that allows us to test
our null hypothesis.

00:19:21.290 --> 00:19:23.720
We're gonna just dive into it.

00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:25.930
Here, in these table,

00:19:25.930 --> 00:19:30.120
each of the columns
represent a regression.

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:35.120
And the bottom row indicates
the outcome of interest

00:19:35.190 --> 00:19:36.023
of each regression,

00:19:36.023 --> 00:19:38.160
the left hand side.

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:40.270
Starting with column one,

00:19:40.270 --> 00:19:43.300
what these represent is a regression

00:19:43.300 --> 00:19:45.430
of the number of incidents

00:19:45.430 --> 00:19:48.880
involving violence against civilians

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:50.580
in a certain township,

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:51.823
in a certain month.

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:54.490
And in column two,

00:19:54.490 --> 00:19:55.510
we're gonna look at

00:19:55.510 --> 00:19:57.910
a slightly different
version of this variable,

00:19:57.910 --> 00:20:00.790
which is the number of
incidents of violence

00:20:00.790 --> 00:20:02.330
against civilians in a township,

00:20:02.330 --> 00:20:04.430
but we're gonna divide that

00:20:04.430 --> 00:20:09.430
by the population size of
that township in millions.

00:20:09.580 --> 00:20:14.350
And this is to account for the possibility

00:20:14.350 --> 00:20:18.641
that conflict is not
linear in population size.

00:20:18.641 --> 00:20:20.523
And lastly, the third column,

00:20:21.410 --> 00:20:22.243
in the third column,

00:20:22.243 --> 00:20:25.070
what we're gonna represent
is a binary variable

00:20:25.070 --> 00:20:26.510
that takes value one,

00:20:26.510 --> 00:20:30.890
if we observe even only
one instance of violence

00:20:30.890 --> 00:20:34.040
against civilians in a
certain township month,

00:20:34.040 --> 00:20:35.840
and zero otherwise.

00:20:35.840 --> 00:20:38.120
And this is gonna help us address concerns

00:20:39.384 --> 00:20:40.950
over measurement error.

00:20:40.950 --> 00:20:42.850
Sometimes, it's very hard

00:20:42.850 --> 00:20:45.100
to keep an accurate log of conflict,

00:20:45.100 --> 00:20:47.060
especially in Myanmar,

00:20:47.060 --> 00:20:49.720
a country where there
is no free journalism,

00:20:49.720 --> 00:20:52.690
and it's very hard to report
what's happening on the ground.

00:20:52.690 --> 00:20:56.240
So we might think that
whether there were three

00:20:56.240 --> 00:20:57.080
or four events,

00:20:57.080 --> 00:20:58.980
is not as important

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:03.000
as whether there was even
one single event in a month.

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:04.690
And this is why these binary variable

00:21:04.690 --> 00:21:06.923
might bring additional information.

00:21:08.700 --> 00:21:11.280
So these things are beta.

00:21:11.280 --> 00:21:13.350
This is the coefficient of interest.

00:21:13.350 --> 00:21:16.043
And what we see is that across the board,

00:21:16.900 --> 00:21:18.800
beta is positive,

00:21:18.800 --> 00:21:22.630
and is statistically
significantly different from zero.

00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:25.810
And if you remember from
the previous slides,

00:21:25.810 --> 00:21:27.690
what this means is that,

00:21:27.690 --> 00:21:32.410
indeed, ethnic conflict
in Myanmar is underlined

00:21:32.410 --> 00:21:34.730
by economic motives,

00:21:34.730 --> 00:21:37.290
and the rapacity effect dominates.

00:21:37.290 --> 00:21:41.170
So increases in the price of rice

00:21:41.170 --> 00:21:43.680
leads to increases in conflict

00:21:43.680 --> 00:21:47.810
in areas that are more
suitable for rice cultivation.

00:21:47.810 --> 00:21:51.120
And this is true for the three
main types of conflict events

00:21:51.120 --> 00:21:52.200
that we consider.

00:21:52.200 --> 00:21:55.217
Violence against civilians by any force,

00:21:55.217 --> 00:21:57.630
violence against civilians
by the state forces,

00:21:57.630 --> 00:22:00.450
and looting and property damage.

00:22:00.450 --> 00:22:03.710
So this is for the country as a whole.

00:22:03.710 --> 00:22:04.580
But of course,

00:22:04.580 --> 00:22:07.880
one of the goals of
the paper is to explain

00:22:07.880 --> 00:22:09.790
what happens within Rakhine State,

00:22:09.790 --> 00:22:11.600
what happens in the land

00:22:11.600 --> 00:22:14.250
where the vast majority
of the Rohingya live,

00:22:14.250 --> 00:22:16.840
and see whether these patterns apply

00:22:16.840 --> 00:22:18.860
in that setting as well.

00:22:18.860 --> 00:22:22.490
So we reproduce the same regressions,

00:22:22.490 --> 00:22:26.230
but restrict it only two
townships in Rakhine State.

00:22:26.230 --> 00:22:27.720
And this is what we find.

00:22:27.720 --> 00:22:32.060
The caveat here is that
the number of observations

00:22:32.060 --> 00:22:33.167
drops significantly,

00:22:33.167 --> 00:22:36.120
and this makes us lose
the statistical power.

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:39.040
When looking at the entirety of Myanmar,

00:22:39.040 --> 00:22:41.680
we had nearly 34,000 observations.

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:44.200
When we restrict our
analysis to Rakhine state,

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:46.960
we have less than 1700.

00:22:46.960 --> 00:22:47.933
And despite this,

00:22:49.054 --> 00:22:53.970
what we have seen is that
beta, again is always positive.

00:22:53.970 --> 00:22:56.060
The magnitude is very similar

00:22:56.060 --> 00:22:59.640
to that observing the analysis
at the national level.

00:22:59.640 --> 00:23:01.140
And in some cases,

00:23:01.140 --> 00:23:04.280
the coefficient is even
statistically significant,

00:23:04.280 --> 00:23:06.290
even though we've lost a lot of power

00:23:06.290 --> 00:23:11.090
by restricting our analysis
to a narrow geographic area.

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:15.030
So we take these as weak evidence

00:23:15.030 --> 00:23:16.430
that the general pattern

00:23:16.430 --> 00:23:19.210
that we've seen in
Myanmar economic motives

00:23:19.210 --> 00:23:22.820
seem to be explaining
in part ethnic conflict,

00:23:22.820 --> 00:23:25.280
seems to apply in Rakhine State.

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:26.730
But there seems to be a lot

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:29.230
that these alone cannot explain,

00:23:29.230 --> 00:23:31.560
and this is what we're gonna try to tackle

00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:32.520
in the next section.

00:23:32.520 --> 00:23:34.450
Provide a deeper look

00:23:34.450 --> 00:23:38.280
into why the Rohingya
conflict might be different.

00:23:38.280 --> 00:23:41.020
And here we've done a
bunch of robustness checks,

00:23:41.020 --> 00:23:44.120
just to show you that these
results are not sensitive

00:23:44.120 --> 00:23:46.820
to small variations in specifications.

00:23:46.820 --> 00:23:48.680
We're happy to go over those

00:23:48.680 --> 00:23:50.590
perhaps at the end of the presentation

00:23:50.590 --> 00:23:51.833
in the interest of time.

00:23:52.900 --> 00:23:55.570
Now, I wanna move on
to the second section,

00:23:55.570 --> 00:23:58.120
in which we show you
vector auto-regression

00:23:58.120 --> 00:24:00.191
just to remind ourselves

00:24:00.191 --> 00:24:03.900
what the purpose of this
part of the analysis was.

00:24:03.900 --> 00:24:07.840
VAR offers a statistical framework

00:24:07.840 --> 00:24:10.360
that helps us understand and document

00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:14.550
how different conflict
variables evolve over time,

00:24:14.550 --> 00:24:18.920
and how different conflict
actors respond to each other.

00:24:18.920 --> 00:24:22.880
The core assumption underlying
this kind of analysis

00:24:22.880 --> 00:24:25.780
is that, each conflict variable,

00:24:25.780 --> 00:24:28.087
say, the behavior of the Tatmadaw,

00:24:28.087 --> 00:24:30.233
the response of the Tatmadaw

00:24:30.233 --> 00:24:32.400
to an attack by an ethnic militia,

00:24:32.400 --> 00:24:34.940
is gonna be a function of itself,

00:24:34.940 --> 00:24:36.760
and all other variables.

00:24:36.760 --> 00:24:38.560
So this is to say,

00:24:38.560 --> 00:24:41.220
how the Tatmadaw decides to respond today

00:24:41.220 --> 00:24:43.170
to an attack by an ethnic militia

00:24:43.170 --> 00:24:45.470
is gonna depend on the whole history

00:24:45.470 --> 00:24:48.857
of past behavior by the Tatmadaw

00:24:48.857 --> 00:24:53.857
and the behavior by the
ethnic militia in the past.

00:24:54.220 --> 00:24:57.340
We're also going to make some additional

00:24:57.340 --> 00:25:01.810
but fairly reasonable assumptions

00:25:01.810 --> 00:25:04.390
to shut down reflection problems.

00:25:04.390 --> 00:25:08.650
The way to think about these
is akin to what happened

00:25:08.650 --> 00:25:10.950
when you face a circular
reference in Excel,

00:25:10.950 --> 00:25:14.250
when the value of two cells
that depend on each other

00:25:14.250 --> 00:25:16.740
cannot be simultaneously be determined

00:25:16.740 --> 00:25:19.000
and you get an error message.

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:20.030
We're gonna make

00:25:20.030 --> 00:25:22.740
just the minimum level
of assumptions necessary

00:25:22.740 --> 00:25:25.743
to prevent these from
happening in our case.

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:31.850
And here's how we're going
to interpret the VAR analysis

00:25:32.700 --> 00:25:33.820
in our context.

00:25:33.820 --> 00:25:37.500
We're going to show
you a series of graphs,

00:25:37.500 --> 00:25:40.540
that documents what happens on a day

00:25:40.540 --> 00:25:43.190
when an ethnic militia attacks.

00:25:43.190 --> 00:25:46.970
How does that ethnic militia
behave in subsequent days,

00:25:46.970 --> 00:25:48.570
whether they keep hitting

00:25:48.570 --> 00:25:50.550
or whether they retreat completely,

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:55.240
and how the Tatmadaw responds
over the following 30 days.

00:25:55.240 --> 00:25:57.960
And this is gonna help us establish

00:25:57.960 --> 00:26:01.760
whether the Tatmadaw treats
different ethnic minorities

00:26:01.760 --> 00:26:03.350
in exactly the same way,

00:26:03.350 --> 00:26:06.583
or whether they treat perhaps
the Rohingya differently.

00:26:07.560 --> 00:26:09.090
We want to be very upfront

00:26:09.090 --> 00:26:11.850
in that we're not gonna
make a causal interpretation

00:26:11.850 --> 00:26:13.013
for these findings.

00:26:14.541 --> 00:26:17.330
We are gonna think about these graphs

00:26:17.330 --> 00:26:20.940
as providing an in depth and useful,

00:26:20.940 --> 00:26:24.950
descriptive account of
retaliation patterns.

00:26:24.950 --> 00:26:27.520
But we cannot make a causal claim.

00:26:27.520 --> 00:26:29.290
Our data doesn't allow us

00:26:29.290 --> 00:26:33.280
to establish why ethnic
militias may attack

00:26:33.280 --> 00:26:34.310
in the first way,

00:26:34.310 --> 00:26:37.853
or why the Tatmadaw might
respond in a certain way.

00:26:39.110 --> 00:26:41.793
What we see in this graph is that,

00:26:42.810 --> 00:26:45.170
following an attack on day one,

00:26:45.170 --> 00:26:49.320
an attack that is perpetrated
by a Rohingya militia,

00:26:49.320 --> 00:26:51.450
what we observe is that
on subsequent days,

00:26:51.450 --> 00:26:53.370
there are no follow-up attacks.

00:26:53.370 --> 00:26:56.330
So Rohingya militias attack,

00:26:56.330 --> 00:26:57.930
and then they retreat.

00:26:57.930 --> 00:27:00.380
We observe that the level of conflict

00:27:00.380 --> 00:27:03.500
goes back to baseline
levels almost immediately.

00:27:03.500 --> 00:27:07.270
They don't engage on additional conflicts

00:27:07.270 --> 00:27:08.710
on subsequent days.

00:27:08.710 --> 00:27:10.670
Now, the graph on the right

00:27:10.670 --> 00:27:14.400
describes the response of the Tatmadaw

00:27:14.400 --> 00:27:16.380
to that precipitating event,

00:27:16.380 --> 00:27:19.160
that initial violent incident

00:27:19.160 --> 00:27:22.828
in which the Rohingya
militia is the perpetrator.

00:27:22.828 --> 00:27:24.480
And what we observe is that,

00:27:24.480 --> 00:27:29.480
on day one, the Tatmadaw
responds to one initial attack,

00:27:30.260 --> 00:27:32.470
one conflict event,

00:27:32.470 --> 00:27:35.133
with 1.5 conflict events.

00:27:36.930 --> 00:27:37.763
And not only that,

00:27:37.763 --> 00:27:39.600
what we see is that the Tatmadaw,

00:27:39.600 --> 00:27:41.960
not only perpetrates at counter attack,

00:27:41.960 --> 00:27:46.400
which is 1.5 times as powerful
as the initial incident

00:27:46.400 --> 00:27:47.320
that triggers conflict,

00:27:47.320 --> 00:27:50.580
but they hit back one week later,

00:27:50.580 --> 00:27:52.790
with almost the same force.

00:27:52.790 --> 00:27:57.720
So the Tatmadaw does not
respond to violent incidents

00:27:57.720 --> 00:28:01.180
initiated by Rohingya militia
on a one on one basis,

00:28:01.180 --> 00:28:04.940
but they respond with
disproportionate force immediately,

00:28:04.940 --> 00:28:06.420
and one week later.

00:28:06.420 --> 00:28:11.210
And they continue to engage
in smaller counter attacks

00:28:11.210 --> 00:28:12.753
for a long period of time.

00:28:13.730 --> 00:28:16.680
And we take these as evidence

00:28:16.680 --> 00:28:20.360
that the Tatmadaw responds very heavily

00:28:20.360 --> 00:28:25.360
to a violent incident initiated
by the Rohingya militia.

00:28:25.430 --> 00:28:28.873
Now, the question of interest here is,

00:28:29.800 --> 00:28:33.841
does this mean that the Tatmadaw
is treating the Rohingya

00:28:33.841 --> 00:28:36.220
in a particularly harsh way?

00:28:36.220 --> 00:28:40.630
Or do they systematically
respond to militia attacks

00:28:40.630 --> 00:28:43.313
regardless of their
ethnicity in a similar way?

00:28:44.170 --> 00:28:45.003
In the paper,

00:28:45.003 --> 00:28:47.310
we look at how the Tatmadaw responds

00:28:47.310 --> 00:28:52.040
to the 10 most important ethnic militias,

00:28:52.040 --> 00:28:54.270
based on the number of conflict events

00:28:54.270 --> 00:28:56.920
in which they were engaged
over the past decade.

00:28:56.920 --> 00:29:01.650
And here, we're gonna present
you a comparative analysis

00:29:01.650 --> 00:29:05.030
of the two most important ethnic militias.

00:29:05.030 --> 00:29:06.860
The first is the Kachin.

00:29:06.860 --> 00:29:09.330
Understanding how the
Tatmadaw treats the Kachin

00:29:09.330 --> 00:29:11.430
compared to the Rohingya, is important

00:29:11.430 --> 00:29:15.060
because the Kachin is a much
more active ethnic militia.

00:29:15.060 --> 00:29:16.320
They are engaged

00:29:16.320 --> 00:29:18.960
in a much larger number
of conflict incidents

00:29:18.960 --> 00:29:21.240
than the Rohingya militia,

00:29:21.240 --> 00:29:23.550
and they are not Buddhists,

00:29:23.550 --> 00:29:25.230
they are Christians.

00:29:25.230 --> 00:29:26.283
So one might think

00:29:26.283 --> 00:29:28.760
that if the conflict
is partially underlined

00:29:28.760 --> 00:29:30.340
by religious hatred,

00:29:30.340 --> 00:29:32.470
being Christian could
be a potential trigger

00:29:32.470 --> 00:29:34.910
for a disproportionate response.

00:29:34.910 --> 00:29:37.270
But that's not what we observe.

00:29:37.270 --> 00:29:38.950
What we observe is that,

00:29:38.950 --> 00:29:43.180
on a day when there is
one violent incident

00:29:43.180 --> 00:29:46.750
in which the Kachin
militia is the perpetrator,

00:29:46.750 --> 00:29:49.390
the Tatmadaw responds immediately

00:29:49.390 --> 00:29:52.230
but responds with the
same amount of force,

00:29:52.230 --> 00:29:55.930
responds with exactly one violent event,

00:29:55.930 --> 00:29:57.913
one violent conflict incident.

00:29:59.050 --> 00:30:01.820
And then we see that immediately after,

00:30:01.820 --> 00:30:03.830
both parties retreat.

00:30:03.830 --> 00:30:06.400
We don't see that the conflict escalates.

00:30:06.400 --> 00:30:08.950
We see that for as long as we have data

00:30:10.100 --> 00:30:11.550
for the following few weeks,

00:30:11.550 --> 00:30:13.830
the conflict goes back to baseline level.

00:30:13.830 --> 00:30:17.523
They respond to one another
with proportionate force.

00:30:18.910 --> 00:30:20.850
Now, what happens with the Rakhine?

00:30:20.850 --> 00:30:25.850
There ethnic Rakhine is also
a good benchmark in this case,

00:30:27.100 --> 00:30:30.770
because they live in the
same state as the Rohingya.

00:30:30.770 --> 00:30:34.180
The difference between the
Rakhine and the Rohingya is that

00:30:34.180 --> 00:30:35.970
the Rakhine are Buddhists.

00:30:35.970 --> 00:30:38.070
They are not ethnically similar

00:30:38.070 --> 00:30:40.200
to the majority Burma in the country,

00:30:40.200 --> 00:30:42.850
but they are Buddhists.

00:30:42.850 --> 00:30:45.790
And what we see similar
to what we observe,

00:30:45.790 --> 00:30:47.157
in the case of the Kachin,

00:30:48.020 --> 00:30:51.670
on days when we observe a violent incident

00:30:51.670 --> 00:30:54.730
in which the Rakhine
militia is the perpetrator,

00:30:54.730 --> 00:30:58.780
we observe an immediate
response by the Tatmadaw,

00:30:58.780 --> 00:31:00.760
but they respond with proportionate force.

00:31:00.760 --> 00:31:03.200
They respond to one violent incident

00:31:03.200 --> 00:31:05.530
with one violent incident.

00:31:05.530 --> 00:31:08.470
And following this bilateral clash,

00:31:08.470 --> 00:31:10.050
both parties seems to retreat.

00:31:10.050 --> 00:31:13.090
The level of conflict falls very quickly,

00:31:13.090 --> 00:31:14.420
back to baseline levels.

00:31:14.420 --> 00:31:16.313
The conflict does not escalate.

00:31:18.530 --> 00:31:19.963
So taking together,

00:31:21.150 --> 00:31:23.693
what these findings suggest is that,

00:31:23.693 --> 00:31:28.650
the way in which the Tatmadaw
treats Rohingya Muslims

00:31:28.650 --> 00:31:30.700
is indeed very different

00:31:30.700 --> 00:31:34.000
to how they treat other ethnic minorities.

00:31:34.000 --> 00:31:36.900
And these seems to be in sharp contrast

00:31:36.900 --> 00:31:40.030
with the argument employed
by Aung San Suu Kyi.

00:31:40.030 --> 00:31:42.720
That the violence that we
observe is not systematic,

00:31:42.720 --> 00:31:45.670
and it's driven by the
whole history of events

00:31:45.670 --> 00:31:50.420
between the Rohingya ethnic
militia and the Tatmadaw.

00:31:50.420 --> 00:31:55.420
Now, so far, our data and
our story has been silent

00:31:55.960 --> 00:31:59.430
on why the Tatmadaw is
treating the Rohingya

00:31:59.430 --> 00:32:01.870
in such a different way.

00:32:01.870 --> 00:32:03.870
And here, we cannot argue,

00:32:03.870 --> 00:32:06.590
we have a good answer for this question.

00:32:06.590 --> 00:32:11.590
But our data set can speak
to one of the theories

00:32:11.930 --> 00:32:13.800
for why this is happening,

00:32:13.800 --> 00:32:17.990
that has gained the most
traction in recent years.

00:32:17.990 --> 00:32:20.793
A growing number of scholars,

00:32:21.900 --> 00:32:26.100
both in South Asia and
based outside of the Myanmar

00:32:26.100 --> 00:32:30.318
have argued that the rise
in Buddhist nationalism

00:32:30.318 --> 00:32:33.250
in anti-muslim sentiments

00:32:33.250 --> 00:32:36.430
in the general population in Myanmar,

00:32:36.430 --> 00:32:41.430
has allowed the Tatmadaw
to systematically target

00:32:41.630 --> 00:32:45.073
and killing Rohingya
Muslims with impunity.

00:32:46.670 --> 00:32:50.270
And one story that seems to be supported

00:32:50.270 --> 00:32:53.570
by a number of qualitative studies

00:32:53.570 --> 00:32:56.630
and even accounts by
journalists in the field,

00:32:56.630 --> 00:33:01.380
is that, a particular form of hate speech,

00:33:01.380 --> 00:33:04.173
which is Facebook posts,

00:33:05.685 --> 00:33:07.810
and nationwide protests

00:33:07.810 --> 00:33:12.810
organized by very highly
respected Buddhist monks

00:33:13.160 --> 00:33:16.700
have helped spark spread rumors

00:33:16.700 --> 00:33:19.990
about Muslims have stoke fears

00:33:19.990 --> 00:33:23.140
about a potential Muslim invasion.

00:33:23.140 --> 00:33:27.490
And this has helped build a narrative

00:33:27.490 --> 00:33:30.360
about how threatening
Rohingya Muslims are.

00:33:30.360 --> 00:33:33.570
And these has not just justified

00:33:33.570 --> 00:33:35.520
many instances of communal violence,

00:33:35.520 --> 00:33:38.780
but has helped the military attack,

00:33:38.780 --> 00:33:43.460
knowing that there wouldn't
be a domestic backlash.

00:33:43.460 --> 00:33:46.170
What we don't know is whether it's true

00:33:46.170 --> 00:33:49.270
that these growth in hate speech,

00:33:49.270 --> 00:33:52.820
and this growing number
of anti muslim protests

00:33:52.820 --> 00:33:56.590
that were organized by Buddhist monks

00:33:56.590 --> 00:34:00.460
effectively predict military intervention.

00:34:00.460 --> 00:34:04.790
We don't know whether these
interventions were followed

00:34:04.790 --> 00:34:08.360
by a crackdown on Muslim civilians.

00:34:08.360 --> 00:34:11.010
And this is exactly the hypothesis

00:34:11.010 --> 00:34:13.550
that our data allows us to test.

00:34:13.550 --> 00:34:18.550
We have a daily log of
protests organized by Buddhists

00:34:20.160 --> 00:34:21.150
and by civilians,

00:34:21.150 --> 00:34:23.230
both in Rakhine State and Myanmar.

00:34:23.230 --> 00:34:25.860
And what we observe
what the data tells us,

00:34:25.860 --> 00:34:30.860
is that for every protests
organized by Buddhist monks,

00:34:30.900 --> 00:34:33.520
in which they propagated rumors,

00:34:33.520 --> 00:34:35.160
negative rumors about Muslims

00:34:35.160 --> 00:34:37.120
or they tried to make a case

00:34:37.120 --> 00:34:40.110
that Muslims don't belong in Myanmar,

00:34:40.110 --> 00:34:42.410
these protests was followed

00:34:42.410 --> 00:34:45.660
between 10 days and 20 days after

00:34:45.660 --> 00:34:48.750
by an increase in the number of incidents

00:34:50.100 --> 00:34:53.080
that are classified as violence
against Rohingya civilians

00:34:53.080 --> 00:34:56.310
and were perpetrated by the Tatmadaw.

00:34:56.310 --> 00:34:59.080
Again, we want to be very cautious here

00:34:59.080 --> 00:35:01.220
in describing these results.

00:35:01.220 --> 00:35:05.200
We cannot claim that
anti-muslim monk protests

00:35:05.200 --> 00:35:08.880
are causing an increase
in the number of violence

00:35:08.880 --> 00:35:12.843
against civilian incidents
perpetrated by the Tatmadaw.

00:35:12.843 --> 00:35:15.920
What we are trying to suggest is that

00:35:15.920 --> 00:35:20.120
these evidence seems
consistent with the hypothesis

00:35:20.120 --> 00:35:23.193
that there is an implicit coordination

00:35:23.193 --> 00:35:26.600
between some sections
of the monastic order

00:35:26.600 --> 00:35:27.753
and the military.

00:35:30.850 --> 00:35:34.510
And now I'm gonna jump to the
last section of the analysis,

00:35:34.510 --> 00:35:38.390
the consequences of the
crisis understood as the,

00:35:38.390 --> 00:35:41.600
by these we mean the
enormous mental health poll

00:35:41.600 --> 00:35:45.430
of ethnic violence on the survivor.

00:35:45.430 --> 00:35:47.160
And for this part of the analysis,

00:35:47.160 --> 00:35:49.110
we are going to use data

00:35:49.110 --> 00:35:52.100
from the Cox's Bazar Panel Survey.

00:35:52.100 --> 00:35:54.350
This a longitudinal study

00:35:54.350 --> 00:35:56.560
that monitors living conditions

00:35:56.560 --> 00:36:00.360
and physical and health
outcomes in Cox's Bazar.

00:36:00.360 --> 00:36:04.590
This panel survey is the result
of a collaboration between

00:36:05.500 --> 00:36:07.070
well, the coauthors of this paper

00:36:07.070 --> 00:36:11.780
and a number of researchers
from the GAGE ODI team

00:36:11.780 --> 00:36:13.190
and The World Bank.

00:36:13.190 --> 00:36:18.190
We ran a baseline survey
between April and July 2019.

00:36:18.440 --> 00:36:19.633
And what we did was,

00:36:20.910 --> 00:36:24.010
surveying representatives
of sample of refugee

00:36:24.010 --> 00:36:27.060
and local households in Cox's Bazar,

00:36:27.060 --> 00:36:29.190
and asking them a series of questions

00:36:29.190 --> 00:36:32.440
to assess their experiences with trauma

00:36:32.440 --> 00:36:33.950
and their mental health.

00:36:33.950 --> 00:36:37.220
And our instruments contain both closed

00:36:37.220 --> 00:36:38.550
and open ended questions,

00:36:38.550 --> 00:36:43.550
therefore, allowing us to
look deeper into the data

00:36:43.940 --> 00:36:46.110
or try to provide an interpretation

00:36:46.110 --> 00:36:48.903
for what the quantitative
analysis was telling us.

00:36:50.070 --> 00:36:53.963
And here is the first series
of the stylized facts.

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:56.150
What we found is that,

00:36:56.150 --> 00:36:59.980
very large proportions
of Rohingya refugees,

00:36:59.980 --> 00:37:03.600
regardless of the year in which
they arrived in Cox's Bazar,

00:37:03.600 --> 00:37:06.610
have experienced at
least one trauma event.

00:37:06.610 --> 00:37:09.853
Here, I wanna bring your
attention to column two.

00:37:11.130 --> 00:37:12.800
What this tells us is that

00:37:12.800 --> 00:37:16.540
over 87% of all Rohingya refugees

00:37:16.540 --> 00:37:20.310
who escaped their 2017
outbreak of violence,

00:37:20.310 --> 00:37:23.860
have experienced at
least one trauma event.

00:37:23.860 --> 00:37:26.770
And nearly one of three of these refugees

00:37:26.770 --> 00:37:30.600
have experienced more
than five trauma events.

00:37:30.600 --> 00:37:32.960
Now, we have pointed here

00:37:32.960 --> 00:37:36.770
some of the most commonly
reported forms of trauma

00:37:36.770 --> 00:37:39.660
they have experienced in first person

00:37:39.660 --> 00:37:41.250
or have witnessed.

00:37:41.250 --> 00:37:46.250
And when you look at the
sample size of the panel,

00:37:46.440 --> 00:37:48.970
and the fraction of refugees

00:37:48.970 --> 00:37:53.430
who report having survived
near death experiences

00:37:53.430 --> 00:37:55.950
or torture, or having witnessed

00:37:55.950 --> 00:37:59.560
the murder of a family
member or a close friend,

00:37:59.560 --> 00:38:01.900
it becomes increasingly difficult

00:38:01.900 --> 00:38:04.750
to believe the narrative
offered by the government

00:38:04.750 --> 00:38:07.450
that these were isolated events.

00:38:07.450 --> 00:38:09.800
Nearly 2000 people in our sample

00:38:09.800 --> 00:38:13.793
report having survived
near death experiences.

00:38:15.180 --> 00:38:17.303
Now, if you look at column three,

00:38:18.490 --> 00:38:22.480
this contains information
about the level of trauma

00:38:22.480 --> 00:38:25.712
to which previously
arrived Rohingya refugees

00:38:25.712 --> 00:38:26.560
have been exposed.

00:38:26.560 --> 00:38:29.310
And what we see is that those who arrived

00:38:29.310 --> 00:38:34.310
even before the most violent
outbreak of violence,

00:38:34.790 --> 00:38:39.330
more than 83% also
reported having survived

00:38:39.330 --> 00:38:41.130
at least one trauma event.

00:38:41.130 --> 00:38:44.820
So even though the fraction

00:38:44.820 --> 00:38:47.980
of respondents reporting each trauma event

00:38:47.980 --> 00:38:49.770
seems a little bit lower,

00:38:49.770 --> 00:38:50.603
in most cases,

00:38:50.603 --> 00:38:53.070
the difference is not significant.

00:38:53.070 --> 00:38:55.970
What we find is compelling evidence

00:38:55.970 --> 00:38:59.260
that the violence the
Rohingya had been exposed to

00:39:00.210 --> 00:39:02.050
are not isolated events.

00:39:02.050 --> 00:39:04.030
And it is highly unlikely

00:39:04.030 --> 00:39:07.730
that so many people have
inaccurate recollections.

00:39:07.730 --> 00:39:09.543
Now, before moving forward,

00:39:10.690 --> 00:39:13.180
we wanted to acknowledge a fact

00:39:13.180 --> 00:39:15.360
that initially caught us by surprise,

00:39:15.360 --> 00:39:20.190
which is, very large
numbers of Bangladeshi hosts

00:39:20.190 --> 00:39:25.190
also reported having had
experiences with trauma.

00:39:25.320 --> 00:39:27.880
These are qualitatively different.

00:39:27.880 --> 00:39:29.070
In many instances,

00:39:29.070 --> 00:39:31.330
these were cases of domestic violence.

00:39:31.330 --> 00:39:35.940
But what we found was
important to communicate

00:39:35.940 --> 00:39:37.740
is that the Rohingya refugees

00:39:37.740 --> 00:39:40.250
is a severely traumatized population

00:39:40.250 --> 00:39:42.510
arriving in a host country

00:39:42.510 --> 00:39:45.580
where the natives have also been exposed

00:39:45.580 --> 00:39:47.990
to very traumatizing experiences,

00:39:47.990 --> 00:39:50.660
and therefore might have
psychological needs.

00:39:50.660 --> 00:39:51.990
And this is gonna be important

00:39:51.990 --> 00:39:55.013
for thinking about how to design
the humanitarian response.

00:39:55.976 --> 00:39:58.490
And this speaks about how high priority

00:39:58.490 --> 00:40:01.610
psychological support services
might be in this setting,

00:40:01.610 --> 00:40:03.900
and not just to refugees.

00:40:03.900 --> 00:40:06.320
This is the last series of results

00:40:06.320 --> 00:40:08.120
we're going to present to you today.

00:40:09.390 --> 00:40:12.710
And surprisingly, what we observe is that,

00:40:12.710 --> 00:40:16.760
Rohingya refugees have very high

00:40:16.760 --> 00:40:18.650
prevalence rates of depression.

00:40:18.650 --> 00:40:22.410
If we were to aggregate across influxes,

00:40:22.410 --> 00:40:23.470
and across genders,

00:40:23.470 --> 00:40:27.090
what we observe is that one
in three Rohingya refugees

00:40:27.090 --> 00:40:30.143
meets the symptom criteria for depression.

00:40:31.980 --> 00:40:35.410
The prevalence of depression
is also very high among hosts.

00:40:35.410 --> 00:40:37.440
One in four, in this case,

00:40:37.440 --> 00:40:39.750
meet the symptom criteria for depression.

00:40:39.750 --> 00:40:41.150
And we're gonna show you

00:40:41.150 --> 00:40:45.480
how this compares with
other refugee populations

00:40:45.480 --> 00:40:47.473
and the general population in The US.

00:40:49.526 --> 00:40:51.070
We wanna be clear here

00:40:51.070 --> 00:40:53.320
that the goal of this comparison

00:40:53.320 --> 00:40:56.460
is not to try to minimize
the experiences of refugees

00:40:56.460 --> 00:40:59.080
who have been resettled
in other countries.

00:40:59.080 --> 00:41:01.210
This is just to qualify

00:41:01.210 --> 00:41:04.410
our use of the word high
rates of depression.

00:41:04.410 --> 00:41:06.940
When we mean that the rates
of depression in refugee camps

00:41:06.940 --> 00:41:07.773
are alarmingly high,

00:41:07.773 --> 00:41:08.950
this is what we mean.

00:41:08.950 --> 00:41:13.950
They are between twice
and three times as high

00:41:13.970 --> 00:41:15.980
as those among Syrian refugees

00:41:15.980 --> 00:41:18.460
who have been resettled
in high income countries,

00:41:18.460 --> 00:41:20.730
survivors of the Fukushima disaster,

00:41:20.730 --> 00:41:24.843
and four times as high as
The US general population.

00:41:27.090 --> 00:41:29.120
And now to conclude,

00:41:29.120 --> 00:41:30.760
in this paper, we have shown you

00:41:30.760 --> 00:41:35.220
that the persecution of
ethnic minorities in Myanmar

00:41:35.220 --> 00:41:37.600
has economic motives.

00:41:37.600 --> 00:41:38.800
We have also shown you

00:41:38.800 --> 00:41:43.210
that the persecution of Rohingya
Muslims is very different

00:41:43.210 --> 00:41:46.800
or represent a very different
retaliation pattern,

00:41:46.800 --> 00:41:48.590
when you compare to how the Tatmadaw

00:41:48.590 --> 00:41:50.800
has treated other ethnic minorities.

00:41:50.800 --> 00:41:53.900
And here we point at a potential reason

00:41:53.900 --> 00:41:56.740
why the Tatmadaw has gotten away with it,

00:41:56.740 --> 00:42:00.170
which is there might be
an implicit coordination

00:42:00.170 --> 00:42:02.460
with ultra-nationalist Buddhist monks.

00:42:02.460 --> 00:42:05.540
And lastly, we have shown
you that all of these matters

00:42:05.540 --> 00:42:10.540
because the human cost of
ethnic violence is very large,

00:42:10.660 --> 00:42:12.570
both on the refugee population

00:42:12.570 --> 00:42:15.100
and on the population
that they're hosting.

00:42:15.100 --> 00:42:19.060
Now we have two highly
vulnerable populations

00:42:19.060 --> 00:42:21.270
living in a very small area

00:42:21.270 --> 00:42:24.577
and competing for psychological
services that are scarce.

00:42:27.219 --> 00:42:29.690
And I think I'm gonna conclude here.

00:42:29.690 --> 00:42:32.810
I promised Teresa that I was
gonna finish a bit earlier

00:42:32.810 --> 00:42:35.210
to leave room for questions and answers.

00:42:35.210 --> 00:42:39.350
We look forward to your feedback,
comments and suggestions.

00:42:39.350 --> 00:42:40.350
Thank you very much.

00:42:41.720 --> 00:42:42.730
So maybe Elisabeth,

00:42:42.730 --> 00:42:44.770
do you wanna get started?

00:42:44.770 --> 00:42:47.100
I don't know Teresa, how
do you wanna do this?

00:42:47.100 --> 00:42:49.310
I would also like to invite my coauthors

00:42:49.310 --> 00:42:52.143
to join the conversation
and answer directly.

00:42:57.490 --> 00:42:59.890
- Hi, good afternoon, everybody.

00:42:59.890 --> 00:43:01.683
Thanks very much.

00:43:04.010 --> 00:43:06.150
A number of questions for you.

00:43:06.150 --> 00:43:08.610
Does anybody take seriously

00:43:08.610 --> 00:43:12.840
the Burmese government's narratives?

00:43:12.840 --> 00:43:14.120
So it seems to me

00:43:14.120 --> 00:43:17.750
to have that to be your
overarching takeaway

00:43:17.750 --> 00:43:22.750
seems, you know, a kind
of oddly weak conclusion

00:43:23.310 --> 00:43:27.820
of a lot of careful inferential work.

00:43:27.820 --> 00:43:30.170
Now, it may be important

00:43:32.170 --> 00:43:35.770
to do that for various policy reasons,

00:43:35.770 --> 00:43:37.230
but it just seems to me

00:43:37.230 --> 00:43:40.490
that it kind of pulls
the punch, so to speak,

00:43:40.490 --> 00:43:44.720
of what you are demonstrating empirically.

00:43:44.720 --> 00:43:49.407
A second question is
whether the VAR graphs,

00:43:50.980 --> 00:43:54.540
they don't seem to me to be capturing

00:43:54.540 --> 00:43:58.623
what on my read of some of
the International reports is,

00:44:00.820 --> 00:44:03.150
attacks initially in 2016,

00:44:03.150 --> 00:44:05.510
then in August 2017

00:44:06.850 --> 00:44:09.150
by the ARSA,

00:44:09.150 --> 00:44:14.150
followed by a wave of
ethnic cleansing violence,

00:44:14.300 --> 00:44:18.230
that pushes the Rohingya away out,

00:44:18.230 --> 00:44:21.080
imprison some, kills many and so on.

00:44:21.080 --> 00:44:23.030
And so that there's (mumbles)

00:44:23.030 --> 00:44:25.250
by kind of seeing one event

00:44:25.250 --> 00:44:29.870
and then okay, it's disproportionate
in terms of the count,

00:44:29.870 --> 00:44:32.080
and it's nice to see a timeline.

00:44:32.080 --> 00:44:36.210
But that's not capturing the
intensity of the violent,

00:44:36.210 --> 00:44:40.090
which is usually the way
disproportionality is measured,

00:44:40.090 --> 00:44:43.640
which we know in this case is huge.

00:44:43.640 --> 00:44:46.770
And also doesn't seem to capture that

00:44:46.770 --> 00:44:49.690
the way in which these isolated attacks

00:44:49.690 --> 00:44:52.930
were followed by these waves
of forced displacement.

00:44:52.930 --> 00:44:56.810
So I'd like to understand more

00:45:00.519 --> 00:45:01.947
how you're seeing those.

00:45:01.947 --> 00:45:04.043
And then a third question is,

00:45:05.210 --> 00:45:08.520
can you disaggregate between
the economic motives?

00:45:08.520 --> 00:45:12.683
So you broke out property
damage and looting,

00:45:14.890 --> 00:45:16.100
but not by actor,

00:45:16.100 --> 00:45:17.180
not by perpetrator,

00:45:17.180 --> 00:45:18.760
whether that's The Tatmadaw,

00:45:18.760 --> 00:45:23.193
or presumably civilian members of,

00:45:24.280 --> 00:45:27.100
I don't know, ethnic gangs
or something like that.

00:45:27.100 --> 00:45:30.333
But under the rapacity,

00:45:33.050 --> 00:45:34.630
you had two different motives, right?

00:45:34.630 --> 00:45:37.410
One is grabbing rice,

00:45:37.410 --> 00:45:39.660
and the second is grabbing land.

00:45:39.660 --> 00:45:43.100
And it would be (mumbles)

00:45:43.100 --> 00:45:47.770
That would seem to be really
important to understand

00:45:47.770 --> 00:45:52.180
in terms of the durability of
displacement of the Rohingya.

00:45:52.180 --> 00:45:55.210
So if you can speak to that,

00:45:55.210 --> 00:45:58.260
that would be very helpful.

00:45:58.260 --> 00:46:01.630
And then my last question is
a question not about the talk,

00:46:01.630 --> 00:46:04.840
but about the abstract,

00:46:04.840 --> 00:46:09.010
which says that,

00:46:09.010 --> 00:46:10.380
there's a line that says

00:46:10.380 --> 00:46:14.640
that the Tatmadaw use
violence against the Rohingya

00:46:14.640 --> 00:46:16.870
to incapacitate the group,

00:46:16.870 --> 00:46:18.340
as opposed to merely deterring

00:46:18.340 --> 00:46:21.940
or responding to attacks
from Muslims and militias.

00:46:21.940 --> 00:46:23.750
And then you say later,

00:46:23.750 --> 00:46:26.010
and that you showed very nicely.

00:46:26.010 --> 00:46:28.220
Then you say the Tatmadaw
deliberately targets

00:46:28.220 --> 00:46:29.740
and kills Rohingya civilians,

00:46:29.740 --> 00:46:33.340
but not civilians of
other minority groups.

00:46:33.340 --> 00:46:34.440
You didn't show that,

00:46:34.440 --> 00:46:37.290
and you know, the record
of human rights violations

00:46:37.290 --> 00:46:40.958
in the north and northeast of the Kachin

00:46:40.958 --> 00:46:43.023
and the other ethnic groups,

00:46:43.890 --> 00:46:47.150
civilians of those ethnic
groups, is very long standing.

00:46:47.150 --> 00:46:52.150
So I'm just wondering what
your position is on that.

00:46:52.650 --> 00:46:53.623
Thanks very much.

00:46:55.200 --> 00:46:57.700
- Thank you very much for
the excellent questions.

00:46:57.700 --> 00:46:59.670
I'm gonna try to address all of them.

00:46:59.670 --> 00:47:02.170
And thank you very much
for the thankful comments.

00:47:02.170 --> 00:47:04.590
So I'm gonna try to go in order.

00:47:04.590 --> 00:47:06.670
With regards to whether
anyone takes seriously

00:47:06.670 --> 00:47:08.600
the Burmese claims,

00:47:08.600 --> 00:47:11.050
they do domestically.

00:47:11.050 --> 00:47:13.100
And to the extent that we believe

00:47:13.100 --> 00:47:15.640
that having the endorsement
of voters and citizens

00:47:15.640 --> 00:47:18.850
have allowed them to continue this attacks

00:47:18.850 --> 00:47:20.210
and escalate over time,

00:47:20.210 --> 00:47:22.300
we thought that it was important

00:47:22.300 --> 00:47:24.750
to confirm that the
international narrative

00:47:24.750 --> 00:47:26.860
should be believed domestically.

00:47:26.860 --> 00:47:29.060
We understand you might
think it's very naive

00:47:29.060 --> 00:47:29.893
that we think

00:47:29.893 --> 00:47:32.830
that this paper might change
the perception domestically,

00:47:32.830 --> 00:47:35.250
but we still felt it was important to say.

00:47:35.250 --> 00:47:37.960
Now, the comment is well
taken in the sense that,

00:47:37.960 --> 00:47:39.940
given the audience we have today,

00:47:39.940 --> 00:47:40.773
perhaps that was the point

00:47:40.773 --> 00:47:42.950
that did not need to be emphasized,

00:47:42.950 --> 00:47:44.132
as opposed to all those

00:47:44.132 --> 00:47:46.080
that would have been a better ending.

00:47:46.080 --> 00:47:48.390
That point is very well taken.

00:47:48.390 --> 00:47:52.470
We're also hoping perhaps this
paper could help make a case

00:47:52.470 --> 00:47:54.900
in front of the International
Court of Justice.

00:47:54.900 --> 00:47:58.890
We think but that this
quantitative approach

00:47:58.890 --> 00:48:02.540
complements very well they
existing qualitative narratives

00:48:02.540 --> 00:48:04.370
that have been easily disputed

00:48:04.370 --> 00:48:06.700
or dismissed by the government of Myanmar.

00:48:06.700 --> 00:48:08.000
So we're hoping maybe

00:48:08.000 --> 00:48:10.870
that helps make these
additional contribution,

00:48:10.870 --> 00:48:12.950
which is a policy-oriented one.

00:48:12.950 --> 00:48:15.153
Yes, now moving to the second question,

00:48:16.420 --> 00:48:18.560
you are absolutely right,

00:48:18.560 --> 00:48:23.560
in that the VAR capture responses
on average over 10 years.

00:48:23.880 --> 00:48:28.730
And therefore the 2017
events are highly diluted,

00:48:28.730 --> 00:48:30.650
and they're to some, again, masked.

00:48:30.650 --> 00:48:33.990
Here, the goal of the VAR was to show

00:48:33.990 --> 00:48:36.000
that there has been a broader pattern

00:48:36.000 --> 00:48:38.770
of systematic violence against Muslims

00:48:38.770 --> 00:48:41.440
before and after the outbreak of violence.

00:48:41.440 --> 00:48:43.760
Because one of the
claims that we're hearing

00:48:43.760 --> 00:48:46.740
about what motivates this conflict

00:48:46.740 --> 00:48:50.320
is that there are sudden
outbreaks of religious hatred,

00:48:50.320 --> 00:48:53.400
and then violence escalates
and retreats very quickly.

00:48:53.400 --> 00:48:55.420
And we were trying to offer,

00:48:55.420 --> 00:48:58.610
perhaps a longer term
vision for what happened.

00:48:58.610 --> 00:49:01.810
There is violence
ongoing most of the time,

00:49:01.810 --> 00:49:03.820
even if that goes unnoticed.

00:49:03.820 --> 00:49:05.700
So the VAR are portraying

00:49:05.700 --> 00:49:08.930
that average level of conflict over time,

00:49:08.930 --> 00:49:12.550
and how the Tatmadaw retaliates or behaves

00:49:12.550 --> 00:49:15.280
outside of major outbreaks of violence.

00:49:15.280 --> 00:49:17.043
But you're exactly right,

00:49:17.043 --> 00:49:20.793
the VAR don't speak to
what happened in 2017.

00:49:21.707 --> 00:49:23.363
That is exactly right.

00:49:24.200 --> 00:49:26.943
With regards to the third question,

00:49:27.793 --> 00:49:30.103
can we disaggregate
between economic motives?

00:49:30.103 --> 00:49:31.760
This is an excellent point.

00:49:31.760 --> 00:49:33.540
Unfortunately, at the moment,

00:49:33.540 --> 00:49:35.840
our data does not allow us

00:49:35.840 --> 00:49:40.340
to distinguish between
rice and land grabbing.

00:49:40.340 --> 00:49:41.770
We fully acknowledge

00:49:41.770 --> 00:49:44.020
that this is a very important distinction.

00:49:44.020 --> 00:49:46.410
This is something we're still working on.

00:49:46.410 --> 00:49:49.360
What we're gonna try to
do to address this concern

00:49:49.360 --> 00:49:51.410
that we're taking very seriously

00:49:51.410 --> 00:49:54.670
is look at existing maps of
what happened to the land

00:49:54.670 --> 00:49:57.720
that has been expropriated
from the Rohingya

00:49:57.720 --> 00:49:59.980
using satellite data.

00:49:59.980 --> 00:50:01.980
But this is an ongoing process.

00:50:01.980 --> 00:50:05.373
It is one that we expect is
gonna take a few more weeks.

00:50:06.959 --> 00:50:07.792
So we don't have it today,

00:50:07.792 --> 00:50:09.183
but it's a work-in-progress.

00:50:10.080 --> 00:50:13.283
And then with regards to the
comment about the abstract,

00:50:14.920 --> 00:50:16.810
that line I think has been removed.

00:50:16.810 --> 00:50:18.540
We have now changed a little bit

00:50:18.540 --> 00:50:21.127
how we explain our findings,

00:50:21.127 --> 00:50:23.470
and we no longer refer,

00:50:23.470 --> 00:50:27.820
we no longer label the
motives of the Tatmadaw

00:50:27.820 --> 00:50:30.560
as incapacitation or retaliation purely

00:50:30.560 --> 00:50:34.080
because we acknowledge that
our data does not allow us

00:50:34.080 --> 00:50:37.640
to perfectly answer that question.

00:50:37.640 --> 00:50:41.180
And then embedded in question number four,

00:50:41.180 --> 00:50:42.400
you're absolutely right

00:50:42.400 --> 00:50:47.400
that our VAR show an
average number of attacks

00:50:49.350 --> 00:50:52.120
on civilians of other ethnic minorities

00:50:52.120 --> 00:50:54.560
that we're very much aware exist,

00:50:54.560 --> 00:50:56.730
and these are completely masked.

00:50:56.730 --> 00:51:00.210
Because in levels like when
you average out over 10 years,

00:51:00.210 --> 00:51:02.710
then these seem to be much lower.

00:51:02.710 --> 00:51:06.650
So this is a clear drawback of using VAR's

00:51:07.840 --> 00:51:10.110
at least 10 years of daily data

00:51:10.110 --> 00:51:13.700
to portray or characterize
other ethnic conflicts.

00:51:13.700 --> 00:51:18.630
We do show events in which
civilians have been targeted.

00:51:18.630 --> 00:51:21.430
And I guess that if we were to
use a slightly different unit

00:51:21.430 --> 00:51:23.060
or a different definition,

00:51:23.060 --> 00:51:23.893
it would pop by

00:51:23.893 --> 00:51:27.043
that these civilians
have also been attacked.

00:51:28.320 --> 00:51:29.690
At the moment, it doesn't.

00:51:29.690 --> 00:51:31.120
I need to think a little bit more

00:51:31.120 --> 00:51:33.440
about how to combine VAR

00:51:33.440 --> 00:51:36.460
with perhaps other ways
of displaying our results

00:51:36.460 --> 00:51:38.670
such that we paint a better

00:51:38.670 --> 00:51:40.900
and more accurate picture
on what's happening

00:51:40.900 --> 00:51:43.240
in other states like Kachin.

00:51:43.240 --> 00:51:45.410
So thank you very much for
these excellent comments.

00:51:45.410 --> 00:51:46.483
I've taken notes.

00:51:47.500 --> 00:51:50.400
We're gonna be working on this
over the next coming weeks.

00:51:57.060 --> 00:51:59.350
Oh, I think we should use that also,

00:51:59.350 --> 00:52:00.900
adding some additional responses.

00:52:00.900 --> 00:52:01.770
Thank you, Mushfiq.

00:52:01.770 --> 00:52:04.253
It is complementing what I just said.

00:52:05.400 --> 00:52:06.233
Yeah.

00:52:10.350 --> 00:52:11.783
Do we have any more hands?

00:52:13.624 --> 00:52:14.793
Oh, I see Hannah.

00:52:17.360 --> 00:52:20.970
Have you found documented (indistinct)

00:52:23.330 --> 00:52:24.510
That's a very good question.

00:52:24.510 --> 00:52:25.870
I want to answer.

00:52:25.870 --> 00:52:27.690
Please let me know

00:52:27.690 --> 00:52:31.130
if I haven't addressed any of
the questions on the chat box.

00:52:31.130 --> 00:52:32.850
I wanted to answer to the question

00:52:32.850 --> 00:52:34.150
on whether we have found

00:52:34.150 --> 00:52:36.100
or documented empirical results

00:52:36.100 --> 00:52:39.900
on the economic consequences
of these ethnic violence

00:52:39.900 --> 00:52:43.410
such as the economic or employment loss,

00:52:43.410 --> 00:52:45.360
presumably, among refugees.

00:52:45.360 --> 00:52:48.740
I would say we have some
descriptive statistics.

00:52:48.740 --> 00:52:51.010
But we are trying to work

00:52:51.010 --> 00:52:55.510
to produce a new set of
preliminary results on this topic.

00:52:55.510 --> 00:52:57.510
We think it's very important.

00:52:57.510 --> 00:52:59.950
So I'm gonna respond on behalf of Austin.

00:52:59.950 --> 00:53:03.000
We have been working a lot
on this part of the analysis.

00:53:03.000 --> 00:53:05.330
The short answer is, we're working on it,

00:53:05.330 --> 00:53:06.753
and we will, eventually.

00:53:09.140 --> 00:53:12.560
- [Mushfiq] So I can just
give a little bit of detail

00:53:12.560 --> 00:53:14.563
on what we find on the economic side.

00:53:16.000 --> 00:53:18.540
So here, you know, the data, again,

00:53:18.540 --> 00:53:21.810
are collected on the
Bangladesh side of the border

00:53:21.810 --> 00:53:23.350
after the displacement.

00:53:23.350 --> 00:53:25.305
And we collected that,

00:53:25.305 --> 00:53:28.200
you know, the displacement
occurred in 2017, July 2017.

00:53:28.200 --> 00:53:30.440
That's when the mass exodus of Rohingya

00:53:31.696 --> 00:53:33.380
moved into Bangladesh.

00:53:33.380 --> 00:53:34.940
I should say the latest exodus,

00:53:34.940 --> 00:53:37.763
'cause there were prior mass exodus.

00:53:41.460 --> 00:53:42.293
And so we have data.

00:53:42.293 --> 00:53:45.760
We collected data on the economic
situation of the Rohingya.

00:53:45.760 --> 00:53:47.410
So let's say the camp population.

00:53:48.450 --> 00:53:49.283
That's one group.

00:53:49.283 --> 00:53:51.460
The second group is Rohingya

00:53:51.460 --> 00:53:55.220
who were already present in
Bangladesh prior to 2017.

00:53:55.220 --> 00:53:58.980
And then a third group is
what we call host communities.

00:53:58.980 --> 00:54:01.200
So Bangladeshis who are
living near the camps.

00:54:01.200 --> 00:54:02.040
And then as a contrast,

00:54:02.040 --> 00:54:03.400
we also have Bangladeshis living

00:54:03.400 --> 00:54:05.700
a little bit further away
from the camps, okay?

00:54:05.700 --> 00:54:08.680
And so the comparison we
can draw from these data is,

00:54:08.680 --> 00:54:10.900
how are the Rohingya doing

00:54:10.900 --> 00:54:14.423
relative to Bangladeshis
who live near the camp?

00:54:15.490 --> 00:54:19.420
Because the survey was
administered to both those groups.

00:54:19.420 --> 00:54:22.680
And the other thing we can
do is retrospectively ask,

00:54:22.680 --> 00:54:24.140
okay, what are you doing right now?

00:54:24.140 --> 00:54:25.050
What do you have right now?

00:54:25.050 --> 00:54:28.860
What is your employment
income assets right now?

00:54:28.860 --> 00:54:31.600
And what was it on,

00:54:31.600 --> 00:54:35.210
like, say, first quarter of 2017,

00:54:35.210 --> 00:54:37.520
before the displacement?

00:54:37.520 --> 00:54:39.730
And so one of the main things

00:54:39.730 --> 00:54:43.310
that you learn from that
data are that, like,

00:54:43.310 --> 00:54:45.450
if you look at the entire
distribution of the Rohingya,

00:54:45.450 --> 00:54:46.820
and let's look before 2017,

00:54:46.820 --> 00:54:48.938
before they were displaced, right?

00:54:48.938 --> 00:54:53.938
The entire distribution in
terms of income, assets, right?

00:54:54.050 --> 00:54:56.560
Look, very similar

00:54:56.560 --> 00:54:58.110
to the distribution of Bangladeshis

00:54:58.110 --> 00:55:01.100
living on the other side of
the river in Cox's Bazar,

00:55:01.100 --> 00:55:03.243
and Ukhiya district where the camps are.

00:55:04.800 --> 00:55:07.714
If anything, the Rohingya had,

00:55:07.714 --> 00:55:09.330
you know, the population density

00:55:09.330 --> 00:55:10.610
on that side of the border is lower

00:55:10.610 --> 00:55:12.760
so they had a bit more assets.

00:55:12.760 --> 00:55:15.910
So a bit more land than Bangladeshis did

00:55:15.910 --> 00:55:18.570
say in February 2017, okay?

00:55:18.570 --> 00:55:22.260
And then what is observed
right after the displacement

00:55:22.260 --> 00:55:23.450
like the current situation

00:55:23.450 --> 00:55:26.060
when we collected the data in 2019

00:55:26.060 --> 00:55:29.640
is that the entire
distribution of assets, etc

00:55:29.640 --> 00:55:31.860
shifts sharply to the
left for the Rohingya

00:55:31.860 --> 00:55:33.750
'cause they lost a lot,

00:55:33.750 --> 00:55:38.420
whereas, in Bangladesh,
it remains similar.

00:55:38.420 --> 00:55:41.240
And so, what you learn from the data is

00:55:41.240 --> 00:55:44.600
one that, like people were,

00:55:44.600 --> 00:55:46.330
like Rohingya and Bangladeshis

00:55:46.330 --> 00:55:47.840
living on the side of the
border near the camps,

00:55:47.840 --> 00:55:51.380
they were under very similar
comparable economic situations,

00:55:51.380 --> 00:55:54.330
were doing similar things
in agriculture, right?

00:55:54.330 --> 00:55:55.900
And then the Rohingya suddenly lose

00:55:55.900 --> 00:55:57.270
all of their possessions.

00:55:57.270 --> 00:55:59.670
And now, the distributions
are very different, right?

00:55:59.670 --> 00:56:01.810
So, the point of highlighting this is,

00:56:01.810 --> 00:56:05.160
one, you know, we also have to communicate

00:56:05.160 --> 00:56:09.359
with my compatriots in Bangladesh on that,

00:56:09.359 --> 00:56:14.359
as attitudes towards Rohingya
are changing over time,

00:56:14.540 --> 00:56:15.610
have changed over time,

00:56:15.610 --> 00:56:18.660
as people have gotten impatient

00:56:18.660 --> 00:56:23.660
with a million people living
in camps in their midst,

00:56:24.280 --> 00:56:27.300
and people are perceiving a
bunch of different problems

00:56:27.300 --> 00:56:31.470
that even though we had
initially welcome them.

00:56:31.470 --> 00:56:34.350
And so, to document this,

00:56:34.350 --> 00:56:37.250
to show to my Bangladeshi
compatriots that,

00:56:37.250 --> 00:56:40.260
look, these are people who
are just very similar to us,

00:56:40.260 --> 00:56:41.240
it's just a bad luck

00:56:41.240 --> 00:56:42.770
of being on the other side of the river,

00:56:42.770 --> 00:56:44.900
suddenly, they've lost a lot.

00:56:44.900 --> 00:56:46.040
And second is,

00:56:46.040 --> 00:56:48.050
something that we need to
do a bit more work on is,

00:56:48.050 --> 00:56:51.450
like connecting what we
see in the loss of assets

00:56:52.360 --> 00:56:54.960
from pre to post 2017.

00:56:54.960 --> 00:56:58.410
And the consistency of the reports

00:56:58.410 --> 00:57:01.920
that we're getting in the
Bangladesh side of the border,

00:57:01.920 --> 00:57:04.120
with the looting result
that Paula was showing

00:57:04.120 --> 00:57:06.673
on the Myanmar side of the border.

00:57:06.673 --> 00:57:09.120
- There is a question
on how we're planning

00:57:09.120 --> 00:57:11.460
to use this satellite data.

00:57:11.460 --> 00:57:13.130
I guess the answer is we don't know yet

00:57:13.130 --> 00:57:15.710
because we're still in the
process of downloading it.

00:57:15.710 --> 00:57:18.420
And we are waiting to see

00:57:19.634 --> 00:57:23.380
whether the quality of the
image is gonna be good enough

00:57:23.380 --> 00:57:25.512
for us to be able to run a regression

00:57:25.512 --> 00:57:27.340
and look at, zoom in,

00:57:27.340 --> 00:57:28.650
and see what happens

00:57:28.650 --> 00:57:30.080
and see if we find an overlap

00:57:30.080 --> 00:57:33.440
between those areas and the areas

00:57:33.440 --> 00:57:37.470
from which our respondents
in our data set come from.

00:57:37.470 --> 00:57:38.303
So that's something

00:57:38.303 --> 00:57:41.400
that we're looking forward
to have our hands on,

00:57:41.400 --> 00:57:43.283
but we haven't quite just yet.

00:57:45.130 --> 00:57:47.970
- And to add to Paula's point, if I may,

00:57:47.970 --> 00:57:50.930
on Susan's question about satellite data,

00:57:50.930 --> 00:57:54.180
one particular avenue as
well that we can pursue,

00:57:54.180 --> 00:57:55.880
and that we're hoping to is,

00:57:55.880 --> 00:57:58.380
with sufficiently
fine-grained satellite data,

00:57:58.380 --> 00:58:01.730
especially if we have time coverage

00:58:01.730 --> 00:58:04.170
after the refugees have fled,

00:58:04.170 --> 00:58:07.870
we can sort of get an
understanding of what the land

00:58:07.870 --> 00:58:12.870
that has been appropriated
from Rohingya refugees,

00:58:12.950 --> 00:58:14.280
is now being used for.

00:58:14.280 --> 00:58:15.113
So for example,

00:58:15.113 --> 00:58:17.680
is it now land that's being rewilded?

00:58:17.680 --> 00:58:19.490
Has it been recultivated?

00:58:19.490 --> 00:58:21.170
And if it's been recultivated,

00:58:21.170 --> 00:58:24.350
that actually speaks to the
potential economic payoff

00:58:24.350 --> 00:58:26.260
of displacing people from their lands.

00:58:26.260 --> 00:58:29.330
So even if we can't use
our economist toolkits,

00:58:29.330 --> 00:58:32.120
we might be able to do
some detective work,

00:58:32.120 --> 00:58:34.520
to speak to the qualitative
nature of land use

00:58:34.520 --> 00:58:35.513
after departure.

00:58:39.854 --> 00:58:41.293
- I want to thank you for the paper.

00:58:42.600 --> 00:58:47.600
For me, the value of a quantitative paper

00:58:49.450 --> 00:58:52.043
and the use of large datasets,

00:58:53.090 --> 00:58:54.840
and remote sensing for that matter,

00:58:56.420 --> 00:59:01.420
is that it knits together
qualitative information

00:59:01.860 --> 00:59:05.210
that we have from histories, commentaries,

00:59:05.210 --> 00:59:09.800
from on the ground, right, observations.

00:59:09.800 --> 00:59:13.620
I'm thinking, for example of
a 20-year set of documents

00:59:13.620 --> 00:59:17.000
that the Korean human rights
group has been gathering

00:59:17.000 --> 00:59:18.070
at the border,

00:59:18.070 --> 00:59:20.450
of every incident that has taken place

00:59:20.450 --> 00:59:22.260
over the last two decades.

00:59:22.260 --> 00:59:24.750
It's an unbelievably valuable source.

00:59:24.750 --> 00:59:28.540
It'd be interesting to
coordinate your observations

00:59:28.540 --> 00:59:33.540
with that meticulous kind of data

00:59:33.590 --> 00:59:37.393
that is available in an archival sets.

00:59:38.560 --> 00:59:40.760
And so I'm interested in,

00:59:40.760 --> 00:59:42.630
there are two questions I have.

00:59:42.630 --> 00:59:46.683
I'm interested in why you
hit on the price of rice.

00:59:47.850 --> 00:59:50.380
I mean, it's easily measurable, right?

00:59:50.380 --> 00:59:53.840
It's available quite easily.

00:59:53.840 --> 00:59:57.740
What other economic variables

00:59:57.740 --> 01:00:02.343
did you you consider and reject, and why?

01:00:03.520 --> 01:00:05.720
The qualitative information

01:00:05.720 --> 01:00:08.540
that I have with respect to Burma

01:00:08.540 --> 01:00:11.150
is that the seizure of land,

01:00:11.150 --> 01:00:14.410
although occasionally it
is for irrigated rice land,

01:00:14.410 --> 01:00:16.180
not just rice land that's suitable,

01:00:16.180 --> 01:00:17.720
but irrigated rice land,

01:00:17.720 --> 01:00:21.820
which is much more
valuable than rainfed rice,

01:00:21.820 --> 01:00:25.090
that most of the seizures and looting

01:00:25.090 --> 01:00:30.090
have been for minerals, right?

01:00:30.100 --> 01:00:33.660
The effort to grab land that
can be used as plantations,

01:00:33.660 --> 01:00:37.773
that overtaking of businesses
and orchards and so on.

01:00:37.773 --> 01:00:41.150
And at least in the
qualitative information

01:00:41.150 --> 01:00:42.600
that I'm familiar with,

01:00:42.600 --> 01:00:46.530
the idea of just seizing
rice land or looting rice,

01:00:46.530 --> 01:00:48.970
it appears almost not at all.

01:00:48.970 --> 01:00:50.520
And if you've got evidence

01:00:50.520 --> 01:00:55.520
that this appears in the
qualitative data as a motivation,

01:00:55.780 --> 01:00:59.400
I would like to see more of that.

01:00:59.400 --> 01:01:01.090
The other problem, I suppose,

01:01:01.090 --> 01:01:05.643
is that you tend to treat the
Tatmadaw as a unitary actor.

01:01:06.560 --> 01:01:08.400
My understanding is that

01:01:08.400 --> 01:01:11.877
the regional commanders of the Tatmadaw,

01:01:12.930 --> 01:01:15.160
and that was part of
the democratic opening

01:01:15.160 --> 01:01:17.040
under Thein Sein,

01:01:17.040 --> 01:01:20.270
was to give them a free hand
to grab whatever they wanted,

01:01:20.270 --> 01:01:23.010
and to never bring any
corruption charges against them.

01:01:23.010 --> 01:01:24.590
So we are dealing,

01:01:24.590 --> 01:01:28.240
as we can see on the streets
in Rangoon in Mandalay now,

01:01:28.240 --> 01:01:32.460
with individual police and
army seizing businesses,

01:01:32.460 --> 01:01:34.200
looting and so on.

01:01:34.200 --> 01:01:39.200
So your question of response,

01:01:40.140 --> 01:01:45.140
let's say, to the Rohingya,
the ARSA attack, and so on,

01:01:46.190 --> 01:01:47.641
there's a lot of your analysis

01:01:47.641 --> 01:01:51.100
that treats the Tatmadaw
as a unitary actor,

01:01:51.100 --> 01:01:53.740
and I think it is not correct

01:01:53.740 --> 01:01:57.190
to treat the Tatmadaw as a unitary actor.

01:01:57.190 --> 01:01:58.690
How that changes your analysis

01:01:58.690 --> 01:02:01.260
I haven't quite thought through.

01:02:01.260 --> 01:02:02.370
The last thing is,

01:02:02.370 --> 01:02:03.433
it seems to me that,

01:02:05.680 --> 01:02:06.513
and this is,

01:02:07.490 --> 01:02:11.720
I don't want to make
this sound dismissive,

01:02:11.720 --> 01:02:13.303
because it's not actually.

01:02:14.410 --> 01:02:18.407
But I'm wondering how many
people on your team read

01:02:20.390 --> 01:02:21.563
and speak Burmese?

01:02:22.810 --> 01:02:26.410
And how much Burmese
sources have played a role

01:02:26.410 --> 01:02:27.570
in what you've done?

01:02:27.570 --> 01:02:29.800
Now, there's a workaround for this,

01:02:29.800 --> 01:02:33.430
and I would like to pose
another question then.

01:02:33.430 --> 01:02:36.750
There are lots of Burmese intellectuals

01:02:36.750 --> 01:02:40.160
and journalists who've
spent several decades

01:02:40.160 --> 01:02:42.640
working on the Tatmadaw

01:02:42.640 --> 01:02:46.750
and trying to understand the
interior politics of Burma.

01:02:46.750 --> 01:02:49.070
And you would be well-served

01:02:49.070 --> 01:02:51.473
people like Min Zin, for one example.

01:02:52.850 --> 01:02:56.510
These are people who I think

01:02:56.510 --> 01:03:01.110
have you run your analysis by them,

01:03:01.110 --> 01:03:06.110
and ask for their comments and criticisms?

01:03:06.980 --> 01:03:09.370
And if you haven't,

01:03:09.370 --> 01:03:14.193
I think it is professionally
a good thing for you to do.

01:03:15.530 --> 01:03:17.130
- [Mushfiq] I was just
going to say, thanks Jim.

01:03:17.130 --> 01:03:18.250
Would you be able to,

01:03:18.250 --> 01:03:21.110
like if we send once the paper is ready.

01:03:21.110 --> 01:03:23.150
So we're, you know, presenting something

01:03:23.150 --> 01:03:25.100
that's a work-in-progress.

01:03:25.100 --> 01:03:26.140
So once it's ready,

01:03:26.140 --> 01:03:28.870
would you be able to connect
us with the right groups

01:03:28.870 --> 01:03:29.840
who should be reading it?

01:03:29.840 --> 01:03:31.290
Maybe forward it to them and?

01:03:33.560 --> 01:03:35.160
Oh, you're on mute.

01:03:35.160 --> 01:03:36.660
Sorry.

01:03:36.660 --> 01:03:37.930
- I'd be happy to.

01:03:37.930 --> 01:03:38.763
- [Mushfiq] Okay, excellent!

01:03:38.763 --> 01:03:39.596
Thank you.

01:03:44.440 --> 01:03:46.150
- Thank you so much for
your questions, Jim.

01:03:46.150 --> 01:03:48.700
It's also personally very gratifying

01:03:48.700 --> 01:03:49.533
to have you here

01:03:49.533 --> 01:03:51.470
because you wrote one of my favorite books

01:03:51.470 --> 01:03:52.967
on economic development,

01:03:52.967 --> 01:03:53.850
"Seeing Like a State."

01:03:53.850 --> 01:03:55.323
So it's an honor, frankly.

01:03:56.320 --> 01:04:00.030
So in terms of what other
variables we considered,

01:04:00.030 --> 01:04:02.960
we actually didn't run
any statistical inference

01:04:02.960 --> 01:04:04.080
on other variables.

01:04:04.080 --> 01:04:08.230
But we did do the intellectual
process of coming to rice,

01:04:08.230 --> 01:04:09.740
and in particular, rainfed rice.

01:04:09.740 --> 01:04:12.000
So that process actually involved

01:04:12.000 --> 01:04:15.360
brainstorming other
agricultural commodities,

01:04:15.360 --> 01:04:18.280
or mineral or extractive commodities,

01:04:18.280 --> 01:04:21.223
that we could use a similar
type of approach to,

01:04:22.420 --> 01:04:23.520
with the maps, for example.

01:04:23.520 --> 01:04:28.520
And so, one set of endowments
that we did consider were oil,

01:04:28.780 --> 01:04:31.010
gas and mineral resources.

01:04:31.010 --> 01:04:33.310
And after looking at the maps,

01:04:33.310 --> 01:04:34.470
it just so happened

01:04:34.470 --> 01:04:37.610
that the geographical
spread of those resources

01:04:37.610 --> 01:04:42.610
was more concentrated
than the rice gradient.

01:04:42.740 --> 01:04:45.720
And it ultimately just became
quite a binary variable

01:04:45.720 --> 01:04:46.680
off of which,

01:04:46.680 --> 01:04:49.720
frankly, it was a statistical
feasibility issue.

01:04:49.720 --> 01:04:51.950
It just wasn't giving us enough variation

01:04:51.950 --> 01:04:53.840
to speak with our causal tools.

01:04:53.840 --> 01:04:54.740
And of course you know,

01:04:54.740 --> 01:04:57.640
that's a limitation of our field, frankly.

01:04:57.640 --> 01:05:00.470
And so we did actually consider this idea

01:05:00.470 --> 01:05:02.247
about rain fed versus irrigated rice.

01:05:02.247 --> 01:05:04.800
And I think we landed on rain fed rice,

01:05:04.800 --> 01:05:06.370
because it seemed like

01:05:06.370 --> 01:05:09.940
one of the most broad-based,
held agricultural assets

01:05:09.940 --> 01:05:14.940
that Rohingya populations would
have access to in the state.

01:05:15.100 --> 01:05:16.610
However, you bring up a great point,

01:05:16.610 --> 01:05:19.170
which is that, irrigated rice land

01:05:19.170 --> 01:05:21.710
may be more of a target for expropriation.

01:05:21.710 --> 01:05:23.560
So we actually have data on that,

01:05:23.560 --> 01:05:25.490
and we could easily run

01:05:25.490 --> 01:05:28.030
an additional statistical test,

01:05:28.030 --> 01:05:30.830
rather than just rainfed rice suitability,

01:05:30.830 --> 01:05:32.980
we could look at rice productivity

01:05:32.980 --> 01:05:34.980
conditional on irrigation,

01:05:34.980 --> 01:05:36.450
using a very similar technique.

01:05:36.450 --> 01:05:38.800
So I think that's a fantastic suggestion,

01:05:38.800 --> 01:05:39.820
and it's implementable.

01:05:39.820 --> 01:05:42.043
So we'll go out and do it, absolutely.

01:05:45.644 --> 01:05:46.477
- Thank you Jaya.

01:05:46.477 --> 01:05:48.190
And I also want to thank James,

01:05:48.190 --> 01:05:50.300
and I wanna speak to the second question

01:05:50.300 --> 01:05:52.840
about whether we were aware

01:05:52.840 --> 01:05:55.560
that the Tatmadaw is not a unitary actor.

01:05:55.560 --> 01:05:58.150
I would say, I think as economists

01:05:58.150 --> 01:06:01.640
we don't have the best
training on qualitative work

01:06:01.640 --> 01:06:02.650
coming from South Asia.

01:06:02.650 --> 01:06:05.870
But I've read a few papers
by mainstream myself,

01:06:05.870 --> 01:06:09.570
and in fact, the part of the VAR analysis

01:06:09.570 --> 01:06:12.440
that focuses on the connection

01:06:12.440 --> 01:06:17.030
between ultra-nationalist
monks' hate speech

01:06:17.030 --> 01:06:18.430
and military actions

01:06:18.430 --> 01:06:21.720
comes from the paper by Min Zin

01:06:21.720 --> 01:06:25.420
titled "Anti-Muslim
Violence in Burma: Why Now?"

01:06:25.420 --> 01:06:27.770
Actually Min Zin inspired

01:06:27.770 --> 01:06:30.659
a very important chunk of this paper.

01:06:30.659 --> 01:06:32.750
In this particular article,

01:06:32.750 --> 01:06:33.583
and I'm gonna refer to this

01:06:33.583 --> 01:06:35.417
because it's the one
I've read the most time,

01:06:35.417 --> 01:06:38.290
and it's more fresh in my mind,

01:06:38.290 --> 01:06:41.400
I think that he did not
dismiss the possibility

01:06:41.400 --> 01:06:45.780
or the argument that the
Tatmadaw is not a monolithic.

01:06:45.780 --> 01:06:48.860
But he said, despite the fact
that it's not a monolithic,

01:06:48.860 --> 01:06:52.059
we see systematic patterns
that are consistent

01:06:52.059 --> 01:06:54.310
with what we have described in the paper.

01:06:54.310 --> 01:06:57.340
Now, I fully acknowledge

01:06:57.340 --> 01:07:00.250
that we need to complement this analysis

01:07:00.250 --> 01:07:03.230
to capture in a much more accurate way

01:07:03.230 --> 01:07:05.340
the dynamics that you have described.

01:07:05.340 --> 01:07:07.740
Yes, there are some
regional dynamics at place.

01:07:07.740 --> 01:07:10.140
Yes, there are elements
of The Tatmadaw response

01:07:11.537 --> 01:07:14.380
that are clearly decided
at a regional level,

01:07:14.380 --> 01:07:17.233
and we cannot at present
fully account for that.

01:07:18.240 --> 01:07:20.030
Now that you've brought it to my attention

01:07:20.030 --> 01:07:20.863
very explicitly, Jim,

01:07:20.863 --> 01:07:22.530
I'm gonna get back to the data,

01:07:22.530 --> 01:07:25.060
I'm gonna have another
look at the detail notes

01:07:25.060 --> 01:07:26.450
of describing the events

01:07:26.450 --> 01:07:28.800
that the ACLED data set collects.

01:07:28.800 --> 01:07:29.650
And I'm going to try

01:07:29.650 --> 01:07:32.153
to think about how we can
address these comments.

01:07:33.410 --> 01:07:36.110
So I'm gonna need to think
a little bit more about it.

01:07:40.490 --> 01:07:41.660
- [Mushfiq] And Jim, can you tell us,

01:07:41.660 --> 01:07:44.920
I'm sure the the entire
audience would be interested.

01:07:44.920 --> 01:07:49.810
So what's your history with
the engagement in Burma,

01:07:49.810 --> 01:07:51.142
and when did you start,

01:07:51.142 --> 01:07:54.142
or what type of work were you doing?

01:07:56.960 --> 01:07:59.010
- You forced me to tell you how old I am.

01:08:01.100 --> 01:08:03.473
I was in Burma before
any of you were born.

01:08:04.490 --> 01:08:06.770
When I finished college,

01:08:06.770 --> 01:08:10.603
for reasons that are rather humorous,

01:08:11.670 --> 01:08:15.840
I had failed to go to work
on my senior economics thesis

01:08:15.840 --> 01:08:17.390
at Williams College.

01:08:17.390 --> 01:08:19.050
And my professor dismissed me

01:08:19.050 --> 01:08:21.640
because I hadn't done enough
work in the fall semester.

01:08:21.640 --> 01:08:22.793
I had fallen in love.

01:08:24.130 --> 01:08:27.360
And I had to have someone adopt me

01:08:27.360 --> 01:08:30.390
if I wanted to graduate
with honors in economics.

01:08:30.390 --> 01:08:31.480
And I found a professor

01:08:31.480 --> 01:08:34.250
who was interested in
Burmese economic development.

01:08:34.250 --> 01:08:38.090
And he said if I work on
Burmese economic development,

01:08:38.090 --> 01:08:39.770
he would adopt me.

01:08:39.770 --> 01:08:41.520
And I said, fine.

01:08:41.520 --> 01:08:43.010
And as I closed the door,

01:08:43.010 --> 01:08:45.990
I asked myself, where is Burma?

01:08:45.990 --> 01:08:47.510
I knew it was somewhere out there

01:08:47.510 --> 01:08:50.560
between India and Thailand.

01:08:50.560 --> 01:08:52.200
Any case, so I wrote a thesis

01:08:52.200 --> 01:08:53.990
on Burmese economic development,

01:08:53.990 --> 01:08:57.557
in particular, the Napin (unclear)

01:08:58.530 --> 01:08:59.880
There was a five-year plan

01:09:00.880 --> 01:09:03.640
that Burma was supposedly following then.

01:09:03.640 --> 01:09:06.280
In any case, I had a rotary fellowship,

01:09:06.280 --> 01:09:08.540
because rather than going to law school,

01:09:08.540 --> 01:09:10.370
and spend a year in Burma,

01:09:10.370 --> 01:09:11.930
fell completely in love with it,

01:09:11.930 --> 01:09:14.220
and would have done a
dissertation on Burma

01:09:14.220 --> 01:09:15.773
had it not closed up.

01:09:16.730 --> 01:09:19.030
And I ended up working in Malaysia

01:09:19.030 --> 01:09:20.760
because the Malay language is spoken

01:09:20.760 --> 01:09:23.350
in four different
Southeast Asian countries.

01:09:23.350 --> 01:09:25.860
And I knew that at least
one of them would be open

01:09:25.860 --> 01:09:28.033
for fieldwork on the ground.

01:09:29.240 --> 01:09:34.240
And so I'm in a sense by
accident a Southeast Asianist

01:09:35.300 --> 01:09:38.300
and by accident a Burma specialists.

01:09:38.300 --> 01:09:40.160
And I've kept up,

01:09:40.160 --> 01:09:41.415
I've been going back.

01:09:41.415 --> 01:09:46.415
I'm working on an eco-history
of the Irrawaddy River.

01:09:48.330 --> 01:09:51.350
And I hope to write a small
book on the Irrawaddy River,

01:09:51.350 --> 01:09:54.620
in which the life forms
of the Irrawaddy River

01:09:54.620 --> 01:09:57.473
play a bigger role than Homo sapiens.

01:09:58.390 --> 01:10:01.810
That is, I want it to be a
deep history of the river

01:10:01.810 --> 01:10:06.810
as a life world of different life forms.

01:10:09.590 --> 01:10:11.020
- [Mushfiq] Very nice, thank you.

01:10:11.020 --> 01:10:11.853
- [James] Sure.

01:10:16.700 --> 01:10:20.100
- [Mushfiq] So Teresa, should
we call the formal conference,

01:10:20.100 --> 01:10:22.210
I mean, seminar to a close?

01:10:22.210 --> 01:10:24.140
- Yes, definitely.

01:10:24.140 --> 01:10:25.290
Yes, we're on time.

01:10:25.290 --> 01:10:30.290
So thank you very much,
everyone for attending

01:10:30.680 --> 01:10:34.387
and especially to Paula and her coauthors,

01:10:34.387 --> 01:10:37.610
all of you for presenting
your results today.

01:10:37.610 --> 01:10:38.443
Thanks a lot.

01:10:39.626 --> 01:10:40.510
- Thank you for having us.

01:10:40.510 --> 01:10:41.510
- [James] Thank you.

